{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qd6kwBzLpKVFFLWMHAr2am","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Core \"DIF reveals benchmark problems\" narrative collapses. Reviewers\n2\t  converge on: DIF is a capability improvement detector, not a benchmark integrity\n3\t  tool. 38% instability = 38% of items are capability-sensitive (models improved on\n4\t  them). ρ=0.307 is the smoking gun for reviewers. GSM1K r=−0.478 is weaponized: \"DIF\n5\t  anti-correlates with contamination, confirming DIF captures capability, not contamination.\"\n6\t  ρ=0.997 trivializes downstream significance. Semi-synthetic injection dismissed\n7\t  as non-transferable to real contamination. Unidimensional MH on 57-subject MMLU\n8\t  called methodologically invalid by psychometrics-literate reviewer. Reframe required:\n9\t  pivot to \"DIF as capability archaeology / measurement efficiency\" paper. Title:\n10\t  \"Identifying Capability-Authentic Benchmark Items via Differential Item Functioning.\"\n11\t  The same data supports a different story — instability becomes signal, ρ=0.997 becomes\n12\t  \"benchmarks are 3× too large,\" r=−0.478 becomes the headline positive finding. Target\n13\t  ICLR 2027 as robust evaluation / measurement efficiency contribution.\n14\t\n15\t  '\n16\timagined_experiments:\n17\t- name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n18\t  plan_id: plan_001\n19\t  description: 'Apply MH DIF to METABENCH MMLU 5,219×12,508 matrix. Temporal cohort\n20\t    (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, within-family confound control, domain-specificity\n21\t    analysis.\n22\t\n23\t    '\n24\t  expected_results:\n25\t    temporal_ets_c_pct: 31.3\n26\t    temporal_fpr_li_clean: 0.318\n27\t    temporal_enrichment: 0.96\n28\t    ability_ets_c_pct: 2.9\n29\t    within_family_llama2v3_c_pct: 61.3\n30\t    domain_spearman_rho: 0.307\n31\t    domain_spearman_p: 0.02\n32\t    sanity_random_split_c_pct: 0.18\n33\t    reviewer_interpretation: capability_gain_detector\n34\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, the actual results hold (these are already\n35\t    observed) but their interpretation collapses. ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) correlating DIF\n36\t    flags with domain-level accuracy improvement is seized by reviewers as causal\n37\t    evidence: items flagged by DIF are simply items where models improved. Enrichment=0.96\n38\t    against Li et al. labels confirms zero relationship to contamination — reviewers\n39\t    read this as \"DIF doesn''t detect contamination at all.\" The 61.3% within-family\n40\t    Llama-2-vs-3 C% is devastating: within the same family (same training pipeline,\n41\t    likely same data), DIF flags 61% of items, which reviewers attribute entirely\n42\t    to capability gain between generations. The sanity check (0.18%) proves DIF detects\n43\t    *something* real, but reviewers argue that \"something\" is capability improvement,\n44\t    not benchmark pathology. The meta-critique about Li et al. labels is dismissed:\n45\t    \"You haven''t provided ANY ground truth showing DIF detects contamination — enrichment=0.96\n46\t    is your only validation, and it failed.\"\n47\t\n48\t    '\n49\t  tables:\n50\t  - name: 'Table 1: DIF Detection Summary Under Worst Interpretation'\n51\t    columns:\n52\t    - cohort_definition\n53\t    - c_pct\n54\t    - enrichment_vs_li\n55\t    - reviewer_counterargument\n56\t    rows:\n57\t    - - temporal_2023_vs_2024\n58\t      - 31.3\n59\t      - 0.96\n60\t      - Capability gain across generations\n61\t    - - ability_quintile\n62\t      - 2.9\n63\t      - 1.19\n64\t      - Minimal DIF = items function consistently across ability levels (expected)\n65\t    - - within_family_llama2v3\n66\t      - 61.3\n67\t      - N/A\n68\t      - Same family, same data pipeline → pure capability gain\n69\t    - - random_split_sanity\n70\t      - 0.18\n71\t      - N/A\n72\t      - Confirms real signal, but signal = capability\n73\t  - name: 'Table 2: Domain Correlation Detail'\n74\t    columns:\n75\t    - metric\n76\t    - value\n77\t    - reviewer_spin\n78\t    rows:\n79\t    - - spearman_rho_dif_vs_accuracy_gain\n80\t      - 0.307\n81\t      - DIF tracks capability improvement\n82\t    - - p_value\n83\t      - 0.02\n84\t      - Significant correlation confirms confound\n85\t    - - variance_explained\n86\t      - 9.4%\n87\t      - Remainder is noise, not contamination signal\n88\t  figures_description:\n89\t  - 'Fig 1a: Scatter plot of per-subject C% vs accuracy improvement (2023→2024). Positive\n90\t    trend (ρ=0.307) with regression line. Reviewer annotation: ''DIF flags cluster\n91\t    in high-improvement domains (coding, math, reasoning) — exactly where models improved\n92\t    most.'''\n93\t  - 'Fig 1b: Enrichment ratio histogram across Li et al. label categories. Flat distribution\n94\t    centered at 1.0, showing no preferential DIF flagging of contaminated items.'\n95\t  analysis: 'The plan_001 results are empirically sound but narratively fatal under\n96\t    worst tier. The reframe salvages them: 31.3% \"instability\" becomes \"31% of items\n97\t    are capability- sensitive — they differentiate model generations.\" ρ=0.307 becomes\n98\t    evidence that DIF identifies items where genuine capability improvement concentrates.\n99\t    Enrichment=0.96 is reframed as \"DIF and contamination labels measure orthogonal\n100\t    constructs — DIF captures differential item functioning, not data leakage.\" The\n101\t    within-family result becomes the strongest evidence FOR the reframe: even within\n102\t    Llama family, DIF identifies where architectural/training improvements manifest\n103\t    at the item level.\n104\t\n105\t    '\n106\t- name: rl_robustness_validation\n107\t  plan_id: plan_002\n108\t  description: 'Validate DIF robustness to RL training. Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO, N=477)\n109\t    and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO, N≈13) cohorts. Compare DIF vs Min-K%/ConStat per-model\n110\t    degradation. Power analysis for partial exploitation.\n111\t\n112\t    '\n113\t  expected_results:\n114\t    n_reasoning_rl_models: 13\n115\t    dif_flag_overlap_sft_vs_alignment_rl: 0.82\n116\t    dif_flag_overlap_sft_vs_reasoning_rl: 0.71\n117\t    mink_auroc_sft: 0.63\n118\t    mink_auroc_alignment_rl: 0.58\n119\t    mink_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.44\n120\t    constat_auroc_sft: 0.67\n121\t    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.51\n122\t    dif_tpr_with_rl_in_cohort: 0.65\n123\t    dif_tpr_without_rl_in_cohort: 0.68\n124\t    bootstrap_ci_overlap: true\n125\t    delong_p_dif_vs_constat: 0.23\n126\t    tost_equivalence_p: 0.34\n127\t    partial_exploitation_detection_boundary: 0.55\n128\t    reviewer_score: 4.0\n129\t  result_rationale: 'With only N=13 reasoning-RL models, no comparison reaches statistical\n130\t    significance. DIF flag overlap between SFT and RL-containing cohorts is 71%, which\n131\t    reviewers dismiss as \"you can''t distinguish robustness from noise with N=13.\"\n132\t    The DeLong test p=0.23 means we cannot claim DIF outperforms ConStat. TOST equivalence\n133\t    test fails (p=0.34 > 0.05) — we can''t even claim equivalence. Min-K% drops to\n134\t    0.44 AUROC on reasoning-RL models, but reviewers note \"you''re comparing DIF (group-level,\n135\t    N=477 cohort) against per-model detectors on 13 models — apples to oranges.\" The\n136\t    partial exploitation analysis shows detection boundary at p=0.55, meaning DIF\n137\t    misses contamination below 55% exploitation rate — reviewers call this \"DIF only\n138\t    detects extreme contamination, which is also detectable by simpler methods.\" The\n139\t    entire experiment is underpowered and reviewers recommend \"defer until more GRPO/PPO\n140\t    models exist on public leaderboards.\"\n141\t\n142\t    '\n143\t  tables:\n144\t  - name: 'Table 3: RL Robustness Comparison (Underpowered)'\n145\t    columns:\n146\t    - method\n147\t    - sft_auroc\n148\t    - alignment_rl_auroc\n149\t    - reasoning_rl_auroc\n150\t    - 95% CI width\n151\t    rows:\n152\t    - - DIF_MH\n153\t      - 0.69\n154\t      - 0.66\n155\t      - 0.65\n156\t      - ±0.14\n157\t    - - Min-K%\n158\t      - 0.63\n159\t      - 0.58\n160\t      - 0.44\n161\t      - ±0.18\n162\t    - - ConStat\n163\t      - 0.67\n164\t      - 0.61\n165\t      - 0.51\n166\t      - ±0.16\n167\t  - name: 'Table 4: Statistical Tests (All Non-Significant)'\n168\t    columns:\n169\t    - comparison\n170\t    - test\n171\t    - statistic\n172\t    - p_value\n173\t    - conclusion\n174\t    rows:\n175\t    - - DIF_vs_ConStat_RL\n176\t      - DeLong\n177\t      - z=1.19\n178\t      - 0.23\n179\t      - Cannot reject H0\n180\t    - - DIF_retention_equivalence\n181\t      - TOST\n182\t      - t=0.96\n183\t      - 0.34\n184\t      - Cannot claim equivalence\n185\t    - - flag_overlap_SFT_vs_RL\n186\t      - Bootstrap Jaccard\n187\t      - 0.71\n188\t      - CI [0.54, 0.88]\n189\t      - Wide CI, inconclusive\n190\t  figures_description:\n191\t  - 'Fig 2: ROC curves for DIF vs Min-K% vs ConStat on reasoning-RL subset. All curves\n192\t    are noisy with wide confidence bands due to N=13. Overlapping CIs prevent any\n193\t    method from claiming superiority.'\n194\t  - 'Fig 3: Detection power vs exploitation rate p. Curve shows TPR<0.5 for p<0.55.\n195\t    Shaded region marks ''practical contamination range'' (p=0.3-0.7) where DIF is\n196\t    weakest.'\n197\t  analysis: 'Experiment is fatally underpowered. N=13 reasoning-RL models cannot support\n198\t    any statistical claim. Under worst tier, this becomes ammunition: \"authors claim\n199\t    RL robustness but provide no evidence.\" Reframe option: drop RL robustness as\n200\t    a claim entirely, acknowledge in limitations, reposition the alignment-RL (N=477)\n201\t    result as \"DIF is stable across training objective variations\" without claiming\n202\t    reasoning-RL coverage. Under the capability-archaeology reframe, RL robustness\n203\t    is less central anyway — the question becomes \"do capability-authentic items remain\n204\t    stable across training paradigms?\" which is interesting but not load-bearing.\n205\t\n206\t    '\n207\t- name: constat_perturbation_eval\n208\t  plan_id: plan_003a\n209\t  description: 'Independent ConStat-style perturbation evaluation on 200+ MMLU items.\n210\t    Generate semantic reformulations, compare original vs reformulated performance,\n211\t    apply FDR-controlled detection.\n212\t\n213\t    '\n214\t  expected_results:\n215\t    n_items_reformulated: 220\n216\t    n_models_tested: 15\n217\t    constat_detection_rate_non_rl: 0.41\n218\t    constat_detection_rate_rl: 0.12\n219\t    constat_fdr_controlled_rate: 0.28\n220\t    constat_vs_dif_overlap: 0.18\n221\t    paraphrase_quality_score: 0.73\n222\t    reformulation_difficulty_shift_mean: 0.08\n223\t    reviewer_critique: paraphrase quality uncontrolled\n224\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, ConStat shows moderate detection on non-RL\n225\t    models (41%) — notably higher than DIF''s enrichment=0.96 against contamination\n226\t    labels. Reviewers argue \"ConStat at least detects something correlated with memorization;\n227\t    DIF detects capability improvement.\" The 18% overlap between ConStat and DIF flags\n228\t    confirms they measure different constructs, but reviewers spin this negatively:\n229\t    \"DIF is orthogonal to contamination detection.\" The RL degradation (41%→12%) is\n230\t    expected but reviewers note \"at least ConStat provides per-model detection; DIF\n231\t    requires cohort structure.\" Paraphrase quality (0.73) is attacked: \"reformulations\n232\t    may introduce difficulty shifts (mean Δ=0.08), confounding detection.\" The entire\n233\t    comparison undermines DIF rather than supporting it — ConStat looks like the more\n234\t    direct contamination tool, and DIF looks like a capability change detector with\n235\t    no contamination detection ability.\n236\t\n237\t    '\n238\t  tables:\n239\t  - name: 'Table 5: ConStat vs DIF Detection Comparison'\n240\t    columns:\n241\t    - method\n242\t    - detection_target\n243\t    - rate_non_rl\n244\t    - rate_rl\n245\t    - per_model\n246\t    - requires_cohort\n247\t    rows:\n248\t    - - ConStat\n249\t      - memorization\n250\t      - 0.41\n251\t      - 0.12\n252\t      - 'Yes'\n253\t      - 'No'\n254\t    - - DIF_MH\n255\t      - differential_functioning\n256\t      - 0.313\n257\t      - N/A (group-level)\n258\t      - 'No'\n259\t      - 'Yes'\n260\t    - - overlap\n261\t      - both_flag\n262\t      - 0.18\n263\t      - N/A\n264\t      - '-'\n265\t      - '-'\n266\t  figures_description:\n267\t  - 'Fig 4: Venn diagram of ConStat vs DIF flags. Small overlap (18%) with large DIF-only\n268\t    region. Reviewer annotation: ''DIF-only items are capability-gain items, not contamination.'''\n269\t  analysis: 'ConStat comparison backfires under worst tier. It provides a foil that\n270\t    makes DIF look less like a contamination detector. Under reframe, this becomes\n271\t    irrelevant — DIF is repositioned as capability-item identifier, not competing\n272\t    with ConStat on contamination detection. Drop from reframed paper or move to appendix\n273\t    as \"related method comparison showing orthogonal signals.\"\n274\t\n275\t    '\n276\t- name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n277\t  plan_id: plan_003b\n278\t  description: 'DVD generation variance evaluation on 10-15 open-weight models. 50\n279\t    responses per item via temperature sampling, bimodal distribution detection for\n280\t    contamination.\n281\t\n282\t    '\n283\t  expected_results:\n284\t    n_models: 12\n285\t    n_items_tested: 500\n286\t    dvd_bimodal_detection_rate: 0.23\n287\t    dvd_variance_auc: 0.58\n288\t    dvd_vs_dif_overlap: 0.14\n289\t    temperature_sensitivity: high (results vary 15-30% across T=0.7 vs T=1.0)\n290\t    gpu_hours_used: 65\n291\t    generation_cost_barrier: high\n292\t    reviewer_critique: expensive and noisy\n293\t  result_rationale: 'DVD shows weak detection (AUC=0.58, barely above chance) on only\n294\t    12 models. The bimodal detection rate of 23% is unstable across temperature settings\n295\t    (15-30% variation), making the signal unreliable. Overlap with DIF is minimal\n296\t    (14%), confirming both methods detect different phenomena. Under worst tier, reviewers\n297\t    dismiss DVD as \"noisy, expensive, and only applicable to open-weight models.\"\n298\t    The 65 GPU-hour cost for testing 12 models × 500 items is criticized as impractical.\n299\t    More importantly, DVD''s failure doesn''t help DIF''s case — reviewers note \"neither\n300\t    DVD nor DIF convincingly detects contamination; DIF detects capability change,\n301\t    DVD detects generation instability, neither detects what we actually care about.\"\n302\t    The experiment consumes resources without strengthening any claim.\n303\t\n304\t    '\n305\t  tables:\n306\t  - name: 'Table 6: DVD Results Summary'\n307\t    columns:\n308\t    - metric\n309\t    - value\n310\t    - stability\n311\t    rows:\n312\t    - - bimodal_detection_rate\n313\t      - 0.23\n314\t      - ±0.09 across temperatures\n315\t    - - variance_auc\n316\t      - 0.58\n317\t      - ±0.07\n318\t    - - dif_overlap\n319\t      - 0.14\n320\t      - stable\n321\t    - - cost_per_model\n322\t      - 5.4 GPU-hrs\n323\t      - fixed\n324\t  figures_description:\n325\t  - 'Fig 5: DVD variance distributions for flagged vs unflagged items. Heavy overlap\n326\t    between distributions, AUC=0.58. Contrast with theoretical bimodal expectation\n327\t    shows real data is messier than assumed.'\n328\t  analysis: 'DVD is the weakest experiment under worst tier. Low AUC, high cost, temperature\n329\t    sensitivity, minimal DIF overlap. Under reframe, DVD is irrelevant — drop entirely.\n330\t    The capability-archaeology framing doesn''t need generation variance.\n331\t\n332\t    '\n333\t- name: ensemble_fusion\n334\t  plan_id: plan_003c\n335\t  description: 'Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble. Majority voting\n336\t    and weighted fusion. Cross-validate fusion weights.\n337\t\n338\t    '\n339\t  expected_results:\n340\t    ensemble_2of3_precision: 0.31\n341\t    ensemble_2of3_recall: 0.15\n342\t    ensemble_weighted_auc: 0.62\n343\t    ensemble_fpr: 0.14\n344\t    uniform_weight_auc: 0.59\n345\t    optimized_weight_auc: 0.64\n346\t    cv_overfit_gap: 0.08\n347\t    dif_weight_in_optimized: 0.22\n348\t    constat_weight_in_optimized: 0.51\n349\t    dvd_weight_in_optimized: 0.27\n350\t    reviewer_critique: ensemble of weak signals is still weak\n351\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, ensemble fusion fails to produce meaningful\n352\t    improvement. The 2-of-3 majority voting achieves only 15% recall because DIF,\n353\t    ConStat, and DVD flag largely non-overlapping items (Jaccard 0.14-0.18). Weighted\n354\t    fusion (AUC=0.64) barely beats uniform (0.59), and cross-validation reveals 0.08\n355\t    overfit gap, suggesting the optimized weights don''t generalize. Critically, DIF\n356\t    receives the lowest weight (0.22) in the optimized ensemble — reviewers read this\n357\t    as \"even the ensemble agrees DIF contributes least to contamination detection.\"\n358\t    The ensemble FPR of 0.14 technically meets the <0.10 target... no, it doesn''t\n359\t    (0.14 > 0.10). Reviewers conclude: \"three individually weak contamination signals\n360\t    don''t combine into a strong one. The fundamental problem — lack of ground truth\n361\t    — isn''t solved by fusion.\" The entire plan_003 sequence (a+b+c) consumed 150\n362\t    GPU-hours for a negative meta-result.\n363\t\n364\t    '\n365\t  tables:\n366\t  - name: 'Table 7: Ensemble Performance (Worst Tier)'\n367\t    columns:\n368\t    - strategy\n369\t    - precision\n370\t    - recall\n371\t    - auc\n372\t    - fpr\n373\t    rows:\n374\t    - - DIF_only\n375\t      - 0.29\n376\t      - 0.31\n377\t      - 0.54\n378\t      - 0.31\n379\t    - - ConStat_only\n380\t      - 0.38\n381\t      - 0.41\n382\t      - 0.61\n383\t      - 0.22\n384\t    - - DVD_only\n385\t      - 0.24\n386\t      - 0.23\n387\t      - 0.58\n388\t      - 0.28\n389\t    - - majority_2of3\n390\t      - 0.31\n391\t      - 0.15\n392\t      - 0.55\n393\t      - 0.14\n394\t    - - weighted_fusion_cv\n395\t      - 0.35\n396\t      - 0.32\n397\t      - 0.64\n398\t      - 0.19\n399\t  figures_description:\n400\t  - 'Fig 6: Stacked bar chart of ensemble weights. ConStat dominates (51%), DIF minimal\n401\t    (22%). Reviewer reads: ''DIF is the least informative signal for contamination.'''\n402\t  analysis: 'Ensemble experiment is counterproductive under worst tier. It demonstrates\n403\t    DIF''s weakness relative to ConStat for contamination detection. Under reframe,\n404\t    the entire plan_003 sequence should be dropped — the capability-archaeology paper\n405\t    doesn''t need contamination detection ensemble. Save 150 GPU-hours.\n406\t\n407\t    '\n408\t- name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n409\t  plan_id: plan_004\n410\t  description: 'Replicate DIF on GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models). Semi-synthetic\n411\t    validation. GSM1K contamination gap correlation as external validation.\n412\t\n413\t    '\n414\t  expected_results:\n415\t    gsm8k_temporal_c_pct: 18.9\n416\t    gsm8k_semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_p10: 0.818\n417\t    gsm1k_pearson_r: -0.478\n418\t    gsm1k_pearson_p: 0.005\n419\t    gsm1k_partial_r: -0.385\n420\t    gsm1k_n_models: 33\n421\t    gsm1k_bootstrap_ci_r:\n422\t    - -0.71\n423\t    - -0.12\n424\t    gsm1k_permutation_p: 0.014\n425\t    cook_d_max: Deepseek-math-7b-rl\n426\t    leave_one_out_min_r: -0.374\n427\t    reviewer_reinterpretation: anti-correlation confirms DIF=capability, not contamination\n428\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, the r=−0.478 result — the strongest cross-benchmark\n429\t    finding — is turned against the original narrative. Reviewers argue: \"DIF items\n430\t    anti-correlate with contamination gap. Models that are MORE contaminated (high\n431\t    GSM8K-GSM1K gap: Phi, Gemma) perform WORSE on DIF items. This proves DIF identifies\n432\t    items where genuine capability matters — contaminated models can''t fake their\n433\t    way through these items. DIF is a capability detector, not a contamination detector.\"\n434\t    The bootstrap CI [-0.71, -0.12] is wide (N=33), and reviewers note \"this could\n435\t    be as weak as r=-0.12, barely meaningful.\" The ΔTPR=0.818 on semi-synthetic injection\n436\t    is dismissed: \"synthetic contamination (flipping incorrect→correct deterministically)\n437\t    doesn''t resemble real contamination (partial memorization, shallow pattern matching).\n438\t    High ΔTPR on synthetic data doesn''t validate real-world detection.\" The 18.9%\n439\t    temporal C% on GSM8K (vs 31.3% on MMLU) is spun as \"DIF flags vary wildly across\n440\t    benchmarks — no stable characterization.\" Deepseek-math-7b-rl as Cook''s D outlier\n441\t    raises concerns about robustness of the correlation despite leave-one-out showing\n442\t    min |r|=0.374.\n443\t\n444\t    '\n445\t  tables:\n446\t  - name: 'Table 8: GSM1K Correlation Under Worst-Case Scrutiny'\n447\t    columns:\n448\t    - metric\n449\t    - point_estimate\n450\t    - 95% CI\n451\t    - reviewer_counterargument\n452\t    rows:\n453\t    - - pearson_r\n454\t      - -0.478\n455\t      - '[-0.71, -0.12]'\n456\t      - CI includes near-zero; N=33 too small\n457\t    - - partial_r\n458\t      - -0.385\n459\t      - '[-0.64, -0.04]'\n460\t      - Controlling accuracy weakens signal\n461\t    - - permutation_p\n462\t      - 0.014\n463\t      - N/A\n464\t      - Multiple testing not corrected\n465\t    - - leave_one_out_min\n466\t      - -0.374\n467\t      - N/A\n468\t      - One model removal cuts 22% of effect\n469\t  - name: 'Table 9: Cross-Benchmark C% Instability'\n470\t    columns:\n471\t    - benchmark\n472\t    - temporal_c_pct\n473\t    - interpretation\n474\t    rows:\n475\t    - - MMLU\n476\t      - 31.3\n477\t      - High instability or high capability sensitivity\n478\t    - - GSM8K\n479\t      - 18.9\n480\t      - Lower instability — benchmark-dependent\n481\t    - - delta\n482\t      - 12.4\n483\t      - No universal 'instability rate' — DIF is benchmark-specific\n484\t  figures_description:\n485\t  - 'Fig 7: Scatter of per-model DIF-C-proportion vs GSM1K contamination gap. Negative\n486\t    trend with r=−0.478. Phi-3/Gemma family models (high contamination gap) cluster\n487\t    at low DIF-C-proportion. Reviewer overlay: ''Contaminated models fail DIF items\n488\t    = DIF measures genuine capability.'''\n489\t  - 'Fig 8: Bootstrap distribution of r with 95% CI. Distribution centered at −0.48\n490\t    but CI extends to −0.12, shown with dashed lines.'\n491\t  analysis: 'The GSM1K correlation is the pivotal result. Under original framing,\n492\t    r=−0.478 is devastating (DIF anti-correlates with contamination). Under reframe,\n493\t    it becomes the HEADLINE: \"DIF identifies capability-authentic items that contamination\n494\t    cannot replicate.\" The wide CI (N=33) remains a vulnerability. The reframed paper\n495\t    should lead with this finding and frame it as: \"Contaminated models show inflated\n496\t    scores on non-DIF items (shallow memorization) but cannot maintain performance\n497\t    on DIF items (genuine capability discriminators).\" ΔTPR=0.818 is repositioned\n498\t    as \"calibration infrastructure\" rather than \"detection proof.\"\n499\t\n500\t    '\n501\t- name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n502\t  plan_id: plan_005\n503\t  description: 'Pilot bifactor IRT on MMLU (general factor + 57 subject factors).\n504\t    Anchor item selection, fixed-parameter calibration for replacement items.\n505\t\n506\t    '\n507\t  expected_results:\n508\t    bifactor_convergence: false\n509\t    bifactor_rmsea: 0.11\n510\t    bifactor_cfi: 0.72\n511\t    bifactor_tli: 0.68\n512\t    n_subjects_57: 57\n513\t    n_items_12508: 12508\n514\t    n_models_5219: 5219\n515\t    anchor_items_selected: 0\n516\t    equating_rho: N/A (model failed to converge)\n517\t    computation_time_hours: 48\n518\t    memory_peak_gb: 180\n519\t    reviewer_critique: 57-factor bifactor on 12K items is computationally intractable\n520\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, bifactor IRT fails catastrophically. The 57-subject\n521\t    structure of MMLU means a bifactor model requires estimating 1 general + 57 specific\n522\t    factors — a 58-dimensional latent space with ~25,000 item parameters. This exceeds\n523\t    practical computational limits (180GB RAM, 48 hours without convergence). Even\n524\t    if forced to converge with simplifications (e.g., constraining factor loadings),\n525\t    fit indices are poor: RMSEA=0.11 (>0.08 threshold), CFI=0.72 (<0.90 threshold),\n526\t    TLI=0.68 (<0.90). The unidimensionality assumption underlying MH-DIF is confirmed\n527\t    as violated, but the alternative (bifactor) is intractable. Reviewers argue: \"You''ve\n528\t    demonstrated that the psychometric foundation of your method is inappropriate\n529\t    for MMLU, but provided no working alternative. Multidimensional DIF methods exist\n530\t    (MIMIC, MIRT-DIF) but you haven''t implemented them.\" Anchor item selection fails\n531\t    because the bifactor model didn''t converge — no equating is possible. The entire\n532\t    regeneration pipeline (plan_005 → plan_006 → plan_008) collapses at step one.\n533\t\n534\t    '\n535\t  tables:\n536\t  - name: 'Table 10: Bifactor IRT Fit Diagnostics'\n537\t    columns:\n538\t    - metric\n539\t    - value\n540\t    - threshold\n541\t    - verdict\n542\t    rows:\n543\t    - - RMSEA\n544\t      - 0.11\n545\t      - <0.08\n546\t      - FAIL\n547\t    - - CFI\n548\t      - 0.72\n549\t      - '>0.90'\n550\t      - FAIL\n551\t    - - TLI\n552\t      - 0.68\n553\t      - '>0.90'\n554\t      - FAIL\n555\t    - - SRMR\n556\t      - 0.09\n557\t      - <0.08\n558\t      - FAIL\n559\t    - - convergence\n560\t      - No (48h timeout)\n561\t      - 'Yes'\n562\t      - FAIL\n563\t    - - n_parameters\n564\t      - ~25,000\n565\t      - <5,000 practical\n566\t      - FAIL\n567\t  figures_description:\n568\t  - 'Fig 9: Bifactor model log-likelihood trace over 48 hours. Curve plateaus without\n569\t    convergence. Annotated with parameter count and memory consumption at each iteration.'\n570\t  analysis: 'Bifactor failure is expected and, under worst tier, becomes evidence\n571\t    that MMLU is fundamentally unsuitable for IRT-based psychometric analysis. Reviewers\n572\t    cite this as proof that \"applying unidimensional psychometric methods to a 57-subject\n573\t    multidimensional benchmark is methodologically invalid.\" Under reframe, this is\n574\t    acknowledged honestly in limitations: \"MMLU''s multidimensional structure exceeds\n575\t    current IRT modeling capacity; our within-subject analysis (plan_014) partially\n576\t    addresses this but doesn''t eliminate the concern.\" Drop the equating pipeline\n577\t    entirely.\n578\t\n579\t    '\n580\t- name: item_generation_calibration\n581\t  plan_id: plan_006\n582\t  description: 'Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged questions. LLM generation\n583\t    with construct constraints, student simulation calibration, difficulty correlation\n584\t    validation.\n585\t\n586\t    '\n587\t  expected_results:\n588\t    n_items_generated: 300\n589\t    n_subjects_covered: 15\n590\t    difficulty_correlation_prompt: 0.18\n591\t    difficulty_correlation_student_sim: 0.34\n592\t    cognitive_level_match_rate: 0.45\n593\t    item_quality_human_eval: 0.61\n594\t    construct_alignment_score: 0.53\n595\t    holdout_panel_rho: 0.42\n596\t    gpu_hours_used: 180\n597\t    reviewer_critique: difficulty calibration too weak for operational use\n598\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, item generation produces mediocre results.\n599\t    Prompt-based difficulty targeting yields ρ=0.18 (consistent with literature range\n600\t    0.10-0.45 from arXiv 2602.06631 — on the low end). Student simulation calibration\n601\t    improves to ρ=0.34 but still far below the r=0.75-0.82 claimed in arXiv 2601.09953\n602\t    — the discrepancy arises because student simulation was validated on simple math\n603\t    items, not MMLU''s diverse knowledge domains. Cognitive level matching (45%) means\n604\t    over half of replacement items test different cognitive processes than originals.\n605\t    Construct alignment (53%) is poor — reviewers argue \"replacing items with construct-misaligned\n606\t    substitutes invalidates the benchmark more than the DIF items did.\" The holdout\n607\t    panel validation (ρ=0.42) shows replacement items don''t preserve model rankings.\n608\t    Reviewers conclude: \"The cure is worse than the disease — DIF-flagged items at\n609\t    least produce stable rankings (ρ=0.997); replacements introduce genuine measurement\n610\t    error.\" 180 GPU-hours consumed for a negative result. Under reframe, this experiment\n611\t    is dropped entirely — the capability-archaeology paper doesn''t need item replacement.\n612\t\n613\t    '\n614\t  tables:\n615\t  - name: 'Table 11: Item Generation Calibration Failure'\n616\t    columns:\n617\t    - calibration_method\n618\t    - difficulty_rho\n619\t    - claimed_in_literature\n620\t    - achieved\n621\t    rows:\n622\t    - - prompt_targeting\n623\t      - 0.18\n624\t      - 0.10-0.45\n625\t      - Low end\n626\t    - - student_simulation\n627\t      - 0.34\n628\t      - 0.75-0.82\n629\t      - Far below\n630\t    - - holdout_validation\n631\t      - 0.42\n632\t      - '>0.90 needed'\n633\t      - Insufficient\n634\t  figures_description:\n635\t  - 'Fig 10: Scatter of generated item difficulty vs target difficulty. Wide dispersion,\n636\t    ρ=0.34. Diagonal reference line shows ideal calibration. Most points far from\n637\t    diagonal.'\n638\t  analysis: 'Item generation is the most expensive failure (180 GPU-hrs). Under worst\n639\t    tier, it actively undermines the paper — reviewers use it to argue \"you can''t\n640\t    replace DIF items, and you''ve shown DIF items don''t affect rankings (ρ=0.997),\n641\t    so what''s the practical value?\" Under reframe, this experiment should not be\n642\t    run at all. The capability-archaeology paper frames DIF items as valuable signal\n643\t    carriers, not problems to be replaced. Save the 200 GPU-hour budget.\n644\t\n645\t    '\n646\t- name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n647\t  plan_id: plan_007\n648\t  description: 'Implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006).\n649\t    Simulate 4 successive administrations. Show power improvement from evidence accumulation.\n650\t\n651\t    '\n652\t  expected_results:\n653\t    single_admin_power_d10: 0.38\n654\t    cumulative_2_admin_power_d10: 0.49\n655\t    cumulative_3_admin_power_d10: 0.58\n656\t    cumulative_4_admin_power_d10: 0.64\n657\t    single_admin_fpr: 0.05\n658\t    cumulative_4_admin_fpr: 0.11\n659\t    fpr_inflation_per_admin: 0.02\n660\t    convergence_rate: 0.92\n661\t    reviewer_critique: cumulative FPR inflation makes this impractical\n662\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, cumulative Bayesian DIF shows power improvement\n663\t    (0.38→0.64 over 4 administrations for medium effects δ=1.0) but fails to reach\n664\t    the 0.70 target from the plan description. More critically, FPR inflates from\n665\t    0.05 to 0.11 across 4 administrations — each administration''s prior carries forward\n666\t    false positive evidence. Reviewers argue: \"With baseline FPR already at 31% in\n667\t    real data (plan_001), cumulative approaches will amplify false positives. You''re\n668\t    accumulating evidence that items changed function — but we''ve established this\n669\t    is capability improvement, not contamination. You''re building a more powerful\n670\t    detector of the wrong thing.\" The simulation uses idealized conditions (known\n671\t    item parameters, controlled drift) that don''t match real MMLU conditions. Under\n672\t    reframe, this becomes interesting — \"cumulative tracking of capability-sensitive\n673\t    items across model generations\" — but the FPR inflation concern remains.\n674\t\n675\t    '\n676\t  tables:\n677\t  - name: 'Table 12: Cumulative Bayesian DIF Power Trajectory'\n678\t    columns:\n679\t    - n_administrations\n680\t    - tpr_d10\n681\t    - tpr_d14\n682\t    - fpr\n683\t    - net_power_gain\n684\t    rows:\n685\t    - - 1\n686\t      - 0.38\n687\t      - 0.68\n688\t      - 0.05\n689\t      - baseline\n690\t    - - 2\n691\t      - 0.49\n692\t      - 0.78\n693\t      - 0.07\n694\t      - +0.11/+0.10\n695\t    - - 3\n696\t      - 0.58\n697\t      - 0.84\n698\t      - 0.09\n699\t      - +0.09/+0.06\n700\t    - - 4\n701\t      - 0.64\n702\t      - 0.87\n703\t      - 0.11\n704\t      - +0.06/+0.03\n705\t  figures_description:\n706\t  - 'Fig 11: Power curves across cumulative administrations. Diminishing returns visible.\n707\t    FPR creep shown as shaded region growing with each administration.'\n708\t  analysis: 'Cumulative Bayesian is theoretically elegant but practically undermined\n709\t    by FPR inflation. Under worst tier, it''s a distraction — reviewers see diminishing\n710\t    returns and growing false positives. Under reframe, it has modest value as \"longitudinal\n711\t    capability tracking infrastructure\" but is not central. Recommend moving to appendix\n712\t    or future work.\n713\t\n714\t    '\n715\t- name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n716\t  plan_id: plan_008\n717\t  description: 'Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement\n718\t    (5%-50%). Validity metrics, theoretical bounds, empirical validation.\n719\t\n720\t    '\n721\t  expected_results:\n722\t    rho_at_5pct_replacement: 0.998\n723\t    rho_at_10pct_replacement: 0.996\n724\t    rho_at_20pct_replacement: 0.991\n725\t    rho_at_50pct_replacement: 0.972\n726\t    reliability_alpha_original: 0.94\n727\t    reliability_alpha_50pct_replaced: 0.88\n728\t    construct_coverage_original: 57\n729\t    construct_coverage_50pct_replaced: 52\n730\t    theoretical_bound_rho_min: 0.95\n731\t    replacement_rate_at_bound: 0.38\n732\t    reviewer_critique: confirms DIF removal doesn't matter\n733\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, graceful degradation analysis confirms the\n734\t    ρ=0.997 finding from plan_017 — removing/replacing up to 50% of items barely affects\n735\t    rankings (ρ=0.972). Reviewers weaponize this: \"Even replacing HALF the benchmark\n736\t    changes rankings by less than 3%. This proves MMLU is massively redundant, and\n737\t    DIF-flagged items are redundant too. Why should anyone care about 31% item instability\n738\t    if it doesn''t affect rankings?\" Reliability drops from 0.94 to 0.88 at 50% replacement,\n739\t    but remains above acceptable thresholds (0.70). Construct coverage loses 5 of\n740\t    57 subjects at 50% replacement — reviewers note \"acceptable.\" The theoretical\n741\t    bound (ρ≥0.95 up to 38% replacement) perfectly matches the DIF flagging rate (31.3%),\n742\t    which reviewers read as \"you can safely ignore everything DIF flags.\" This experiment\n743\t    depends on plan_005/006 (which fail), so the \"replacement\" is simulated via random\n744\t    item substitution rather than actual generated items — further reducing credibility.\n745\t\n746\t    '\n747\t  tables:\n748\t  - name: 'Table 13: Graceful Degradation Curves'\n749\t    columns:\n750\t    - replacement_pct\n751\t    - rank_rho\n752\t    - reliability_alpha\n753\t    - subjects_covered\n754\t    - verdict\n755\t    rows:\n756\t    - - 0\n757\t      - 1.0\n758\t      - 0.94\n759\t      - 57\n760\t      - baseline\n761\t    - - 5\n762\t      - 0.998\n763\t      - 0.93\n764\t      - 57\n765\t      - negligible\n766\t    - - 10\n767\t      - 0.996\n768\t      - 0.93\n769\t      - 57\n770\t      - negligible\n771\t    - - 20\n772\t      - 0.991\n773\t      - 0.91\n774\t      - 56\n775\t      - minor\n776\t    - - 31\n777\t      - 0.987\n778\t      - 0.9\n779\t      - 55\n780\t      - DIF removal point\n781\t    - - 50\n782\t      - 0.972\n783\t      - 0.88\n784\t      - 52\n785\t      - still acceptable\n786\t  figures_description:\n787\t  - 'Fig 12: Degradation curve showing ρ vs replacement rate. Nearly flat until 40%,\n788\t    gentle decline thereafter. DIF removal rate (31%) marked — well within ''safe''\n789\t    zone. Reviewer: ''This graph argues against your own paper.'''\n790\t  analysis: 'Graceful degradation is self-defeating under worst tier AND original\n791\t    framing. It proves removing DIF items doesn''t matter, undermining significance.\n792\t    Under reframe, this becomes: \"MMLU is 3× larger than needed for stable rankings\n793\t    — DIF identifies the informative core (the items that DO contribute discriminative\n794\t    signal).\" The ρ=0.972 at 50% replacement becomes \"you could cut MMLU in half and\n795\t    preserve rankings — DIF tells you which half to keep.\" This is actually a strong\n796\t    reframe, turning the worst finding into a measurement efficiency contribution.\n797\t\n798\t    '\n799\t- name: item_focused_trees\n800\t  plan_id: plan_009\n801\t  description: 'Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) as alternative to categorical\n802\t    cohort DIF. Continuous covariate (release date). Compare detection power vs categorical\n803\t    MH.\n804\t\n805\t    '\n806\t  expected_results:\n807\t    ift_detection_rate: 0.34\n808\t    ift_vs_mh_overlap: 0.78\n809\t    ift_unique_flags: 0.06\n810\t    mh_unique_flags: 0.04\n811\t    ift_uses_full_sample: true\n812\t    ift_avoids_cohort_splits: true\n813\t    ift_computational_cost_ratio: 12.0\n814\t    ift_release_date_coefficient_mean: 0.08\n815\t    ift_release_date_coefficient_std: 0.15\n816\t    reviewer_critique: confirms temporal trend = capability gain\n817\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, Item-Focused Trees detect 34% of items as having\n818\t    significant temporal DIF — nearly identical to MH''s 31.3%. The 78% overlap confirms\n819\t    both methods detect the same phenomenon. The IFT release-date coefficient (mean=0.08,\n820\t    SD=0.15) is positive: items become easier over time, consistent with capability\n821\t    improvement. Reviewers read the continuous coefficient as: \"DIF is a smooth function\n822\t    of time, not a discrete shift — this is exactly what capability improvement looks\n823\t    like. If contamination caused DIF, we''d expect discrete jumps when specific training\n824\t    data became available.\" The 12× computational cost over MH makes IFT impractical\n825\t    at scale. Reviewers conclude: \"IFT confirms MH findings but adds computational\n826\t    cost and the interpretation that item changes are gradual (capability) not abrupt\n827\t    (contamination).\"\n828\t\n829\t    '\n830\t  tables:\n831\t  - name: 'Table 14: IFT vs MH Comparison'\n832\t    columns:\n833\t    - method\n834\t    - flagging_rate\n835\t    - unique_flags\n836\t    - cost_ratio\n837\t    - temporal_pattern\n838\t    rows:\n839\t    - - MH_categorical\n840\t      - 0.313\n841\t      - 0.04\n842\t      - 1.0\n843\t      - Discrete cohort split\n844\t    - - IFT_continuous\n845\t      - 0.34\n846\t      - 0.06\n847\t      - 12.0\n848\t      - Smooth positive trend\n849\t    - - overlap\n850\t      - 0.78\n851\t      - '-'\n852\t      - '-'\n853\t      - Same underlying signal\n854\t  figures_description:\n855\t  - 'Fig 13: IFT temporal coefficient distribution. Positive skew (mean=0.08), indicating\n856\t    items become easier over time. Reviewer: ''Gradual easing = capability improvement.'''\n857\t  analysis: 'IFT provides marginal methodological value but under worst tier confirms\n858\t    the capability-improvement interpretation via smooth temporal trends. Under reframe,\n859\t    the smooth trend is actually interesting: \"capability-authentic items show predictable\n860\t    sensitivity to model generation — enabling prospective identification of items\n861\t    that will become less discriminative.\" Recommend as supplementary/appendix rather\n862\t    than main paper contribution.\n863\t\n864\t    '\n865\t- name: architecture_stratified_dif\n866\t  plan_id: plan_010\n867\t  description: 'DIF within decoder-only homogeneous subpopulation. IRT fit diagnostics.\n868\t    Compare FPR/TPR vs full-population DIF from plan_001.\n869\t\n870\t    '\n871\t  expected_results:\n872\t    n_decoder_only: 3400\n873\t    decoder_only_c_pct: 33.1\n874\t    decoder_only_irt_rmsea: 0.092\n875\t    decoder_only_irt_cfi: 0.81\n876\t    decoder_only_irt_tli: 0.78\n877\t    decoder_only_irt_srmr: 0.073\n878\t    full_population_c_pct: 31.3\n879\t    delta_c_pct: 1.8\n880\t    fpr_decoder_only: 0.33\n881\t    fpr_full_population: 0.318\n882\t    tpr_delta_p05: -0.02\n883\t    reviewer_critique: architecture homogeneity doesn't fix the problem\n884\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier (explicitly specified in tier definition), architecture\n885\t    stratification fails to improve results. Decoder-only C% = 33.1% (vs 31.3% full\n886\t    population) — no meaningful reduction, slightly HIGHER. IRT fit remains poor:\n887\t    RMSEA=0.092 (>0.08), CFI=0.81 (<0.90), TLI=0.78 (<0.90). Removing encoder-decoder\n888\t    and hybrid architectures doesn''t address the fundamental unidimensionality problem\n889\t    — MMLU''s 57-subject structure violates unidimensional IRT regardless of architecture\n890\t    homogeneity. The C% increase from 31.3%→33.1% is within noise but reviewers spin\n891\t    it: \"architecture stratification made things WORSE — DIF flags more items in a\n892\t    homogeneous population, confirming that flags reflect capability variation, not\n893\t    architecture confounding.\" FPR and TPR are essentially unchanged (ΔFPR=+0.01,\n894\t    ΔTPR=−0.02). Reviewers familiar with psychometrics argue: \"RMSEA=0.092 on decoder-only\n895\t    models proves the IRT model is misspecified for MMLU. Your entire DIF analysis\n896\t    rests on an inappropriate measurement model. This is not fixable by subsetting\n897\t    — it requires multidimensional IRT, which you haven''t implemented.\"\n898\t\n899\t    '\n900\t  tables:\n901\t  - name: 'Table 15: Architecture Stratification Results'\n902\t    columns:\n903\t    - population\n904\t    - n_models\n905\t    - c_pct\n906\t    - rmsea\n907\t    - cfi\n908\t    - tli\n909\t    rows:\n910\t    - - full_population\n911\t      - 5219\n912\t      - 31.3\n913\t      - N/A (not computed)\n914\t      - N/A\n915\t      - N/A\n916\t    - - decoder_only\n917\t      - 3400\n918\t      - 33.1\n919\t      - 0.092\n920\t      - 0.81\n921\t      - 0.78\n922\t    - - delta\n923\t      - '-1819'\n924\t      - '+1.8'\n925\t      - FAIL (>0.08)\n926\t      - FAIL (<0.90)\n927\t      - FAIL (<0.90)\n928\t  - name: 'Table 16: Detection Power Comparison'\n929\t    columns:\n930\t    - population\n931\t    - fpr_baseline\n932\t    - tpr_p05\n933\t    - tpr_p10\n934\t    - enrichment\n935\t    rows:\n936\t    - - full_population\n937\t      - 0.318\n938\t      - 0.834\n939\t      - 1.0\n940\t      - 0.96\n941\t    - - decoder_only\n942\t      - 0.33\n943\t      - 0.82\n944\t      - 0.99\n945\t      - 0.95\n946\t    - - delta\n947\t      - '+0.012'\n948\t      - '-0.014'\n949\t      - '-0.01'\n950\t      - '-0.01'\n951\t  figures_description:\n952\t  - 'Fig 14: IRT fit indices for decoder-only vs full population. All indices remain\n953\t    below acceptable thresholds. RMSEA shown with 90% CI entirely above 0.08 cutoff.'\n954\t  - 'Fig 15: DIF flagging rate comparison. Near-identical distributions between full\n955\t    and decoder-only populations, disproving the architecture confound hypothesis.'\n956\t  analysis: 'Architecture stratification is the critical missing experiment identified\n957\t    by worst-case Dream, and it produces the predicted negative result. Under worst\n958\t    tier, this closes the last escape route: DIF flagging rates cannot be reduced\n959\t    by controlling for architecture. The IRT misfit is structural (MMLU dimensionality)\n960\t    not population-driven. Under reframe, this becomes neutral: \"DIF item sensitivity\n961\t    is robust to population composition — capability-authentic items are identified\n962\t    regardless of architecture mix.\" The IRT fit concern is acknowledged honestly\n963\t    but framed as \"conservative: poor IRT fit means DIF may UNDERESTIMATE the true\n964\t    proportion of capability-sensitive items.\"\n965\t\n966\t    '\n967\t- name: semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n968\t  plan_id: plan_011\n969\t  description: 'Inject known contamination into METABENCH response matrix. Measure\n970\t    DIF TPR/FPR against controlled ground truth. Vary injection parameters.\n971\t\n972\t    '\n973\t  expected_results:\n974\t    delta_tpr_p03: 0.222\n975\t    delta_tpr_p05: 0.508\n976\t    delta_tpr_p07: 0.664\n977\t    delta_tpr_p10: 0.674\n978\t    external_matching_fpr: 0.314\n979\t    within_matching_fpr_inflation: 0.186\n980\t    cross_subject_delta_fpr: 0.0\n981\t    cross_subject_delta_tpr_p05_avg: 0.36\n982\t    dose_response_present: true\n983\t    reviewer_critique: synthetic injection mechanism unrealistic\n984\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, semi-synthetic results (already observed) are\n985\t    technically sound but dismissed on ecological validity grounds. ΔTPR=0.508 at\n986\t    p=0.5 shows DIF detects injected contamination — but reviewers argue the injection\n987\t    mechanism (flip incorrect→correct with probability p) doesn''t model real contamination.\n988\t    Real contamination involves: (1) partial memorization (model recalls fragments,\n989\t    not full answers), (2) shallow pattern matching (model exploits surface features),\n990\t    (3) chain-of-thought reasoning from memorized premises. None of these produce\n991\t    the clean incorrect→correct flipping that synthetic injection assumes. The dose-response\n992\t    curve (ΔTPR increases with p) is called \"trivially expected — of course flipping\n993\t    more answers makes items behave more differently.\" The cross- subject matching\n994\t    innovation (ΔFPR=0.000) is acknowledged as methodologically sound, but reviewers\n995\t    argue \"you''ve built a valid framework for detecting something that doesn''t exist\n996\t    in the real world in this form.\" The gap between synthetic ΔTPR=0.508 and real\n997\t    enrichment=0.96 is the fatal disconnect: \"Your method works perfectly on synthetic\n998\t    contamination and not at all on real data — this means your synthetic model is\n999\t    wrong, not that your ground truth is wrong.\"\n1000\t\n1001\t    '\n1002\t  tables:\n1003\t  - name: 'Table 17: Semi-Synthetic Results Under Worst Interpretation'\n1004\t    columns:\n1005\t    - injection_rate_p\n1006\t    - delta_tpr_standard\n1007\t    - delta_tpr_cross_subject\n1008\t    - reviewer_spin\n1009\t    rows:\n1010\t    - - 0.3\n1011\t      - 0.222\n1012\t      - 0.18\n1013\t      - Below useful detection\n1014\t    - - 0.5\n1015\t      - 0.508\n1016\t      - 0.36\n1017\t      - Only works at 50%+ exploitation\n1018\t    - - 0.7\n1019\t      - 0.664\n1020\t      - 0.52\n1021\t      - High exploitation = easy detection\n1022\t    - - 1.0\n1023\t      - 0.674\n1024\t      - 0.62\n1025\t      - Saturates — ceiling effect\n1026\t  - name: 'Table 18: Matching Strategy Comparison'\n1027\t    columns:\n1028\t    - matching\n1029\t    - fpr_baseline\n1030\t    - fpr_at_p10\n1031\t    - delta_fpr\n1032\t    - verdict\n1033\t    rows:\n1034\t    - - standard_MH\n1035\t      - 0.298\n1036\t      - 0.616\n1037\t      - '+0.318'\n1038\t      - FPR inflates (contaminated matching var)\n1039\t    - - external_5subj\n1040\t      - 0.314\n1041\t      - 0.314\n1042\t      - 0.0\n1043\t      - FPR stable (clean matching var)\n1044\t    - - cross_subject\n1045\t      - 0.33\n1046\t      - 0.33\n1047\t      - 0.0\n1048\t      - FPR stable (recommended)\n1049\t  figures_description:\n1050\t  - 'Fig 16: Dose-response curve. ΔTPR vs injection rate p. Monotonic increase but\n1051\t    with diminishing returns. Reviewer annotation: ''Detection requires >50% exploitation\n1052\t    — real contamination is partial.'''\n1053\t  - 'Fig 17: FPR comparison across matching strategies. Standard MH shows inflation;\n1054\t    cross-subject matching maintains stable FPR. The methodological contribution is\n1055\t    acknowledged even under worst tier.'\n1056\t  analysis: Semi-synthetic validation is the strongest methodological contribution\n1057\t    even under worst tier. The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000) is genuinely\n1058\t    novel and useful. Under reframe, the semi-synthetic framework is repositioned\n1059\t    as \"calibration infrastructure for item sensitivity analysis\" — it characterizes\n1060\t    how much differential signal is needed before DIF detects it, without claiming\n1061\t    this maps to real contamination. The dose-response curve becomes \"sensitivity\n1062\t    calibration\" rather than \"contamination detection power.\" This experiment survives\n1063\t    the reframe relatively intact.\n1064\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f077ac60-b5f3-45fc-82ab-a50de5ad3465","uuid":"557d5236-e924-4727-83ed-9e855b939443","timestamp":"2026-05-21T02:15:53.844Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T012912/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Core \"DIF reveals benchmark problems\" narrative collapses. Reviewers\n  converge on: DIF is a capability improvement detector, not a benchmark integrity\n  tool. 38% instability = 38% of items are capability-sensitive (models improved on\n  them). ρ=0.307 is the smoking gun for reviewers. GSM1K r=−0.478 is weaponized: \"DIF\n  anti-correlates with contamination, confirming DIF captures capability, not contamination.\"\n  ρ=0.997 trivializes downstream significance. Semi-synthetic injection dismissed\n  as non-transferable to real contamination. Unidimensional MH on 57-subject MMLU\n  called methodologically invalid by psychometrics-literate reviewer. Reframe required:\n  pivot to \"DIF as capability archaeology / measurement efficiency\" paper. Title:\n  \"Identifying Capability-Authentic Benchmark Items via Differential Item Functioning.\"\n  The same data supports a different story — instability becomes signal, ρ=0.997 becomes\n  \"benchmarks are 3× too large,\" r=−0.478 becomes the headline positive finding. Target\n  ICLR 2027 as robust evaluation / measurement efficiency contribution.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: 'Apply MH DIF to METABENCH MMLU 5,219×12,508 matrix. Temporal cohort\n    (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, within-family confound control, domain-specificity\n    analysis.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    temporal_ets_c_pct: 31.3\n    temporal_fpr_li_clean: 0.318\n    temporal_enrichment: 0.96\n    ability_ets_c_pct: 2.9\n    within_family_llama2v3_c_pct: 61.3\n    domain_spearman_rho: 0.307\n    domain_spearman_p: 0.02\n    sanity_random_split_c_pct: 0.18\n    reviewer_interpretation: capability_gain_detector\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, the actual results hold (these are already\n    observed) but their interpretation collapses. ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) correlating DIF\n    flags with domain-level accuracy improvement is seized by reviewers as causal\n    evidence: items flagged by DIF are simply items where models improved. Enrichment=0.96\n    against Li et al. labels confirms zero relationship to contamination — reviewers\n    read this as \"DIF doesn''t detect contamination at all.\" The 61.3% within-family\n    Llama-2-vs-3 C% is devastating: within the same family (same training pipeline,\n    likely same data), DIF flags 61% of items, which reviewers attribute entirely\n    to capability gain between generations. The sanity check (0.18%) proves DIF detects\n    *something* real, but reviewers argue that \"something\" is capability improvement,\n    not benchmark pathology. The meta-critique about Li et al. labels is dismissed:\n    \"You haven''t provided ANY ground truth showing DIF detects contamination — enrichment=0.96\n    is your only validation, and it failed.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: DIF Detection Summary Under Worst Interpretation'\n    columns:\n    - cohort_definition\n    - c_pct\n    - enrichment_vs_li\n    - reviewer_counterargument\n    rows:\n    - - temporal_2023_vs_2024\n      - 31.3\n      - 0.96\n      - Capability gain across generations\n    - - ability_quintile\n      - 2.9\n      - 1.19\n      - Minimal DIF = items function consistently across ability levels (expected)\n    - - within_family_llama2v3\n      - 61.3\n      - N/A\n      - Same family, same data pipeline → pure capability gain\n    - - random_split_sanity\n      - 0.18\n      - N/A\n      - Confirms real signal, but signal = capability\n  - name: 'Table 2: Domain Correlation Detail'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - value\n    - reviewer_spin\n    rows:\n    - - spearman_rho_dif_vs_accuracy_gain\n      - 0.307\n      - DIF tracks capability improvement\n    - - p_value\n      - 0.02\n      - Significant correlation confirms confound\n    - - variance_explained\n      - 9.4%\n      - Remainder is noise, not contamination signal\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 1a: Scatter plot of per-subject C% vs accuracy improvement (2023→2024). Positive\n    trend (ρ=0.307) with regression line. Reviewer annotation: ''DIF flags cluster\n    in high-improvement domains (coding, math, reasoning) — exactly where models improved\n    most.'''\n  - 'Fig 1b: Enrichment ratio histogram across Li et al. label categories. Flat distribution\n    centered at 1.0, showing no preferential DIF flagging of contaminated items.'\n  analysis: 'The plan_001 results are empirically sound but narratively fatal under\n    worst tier. The reframe salvages them: 31.3% \"instability\" becomes \"31% of items\n    are capability- sensitive — they differentiate model generations.\" ρ=0.307 becomes\n    evidence that DIF identifies items where genuine capability improvement concentrates.\n    Enrichment=0.96 is reframed as \"DIF and contamination labels measure orthogonal\n    constructs — DIF captures differential item functioning, not data leakage.\" The\n    within-family result becomes the strongest evidence FOR the reframe: even within\n    Llama family, DIF identifies where architectural/training improvements manifest\n    at the item level.\n\n    '\n- name: rl_robustness_validation\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Validate DIF robustness to RL training. Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO, N=477)\n    and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO, N≈13) cohorts. Compare DIF vs Min-K%/ConStat per-model\n    degradation. Power analysis for partial exploitation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_reasoning_rl_models: 13\n    dif_flag_overlap_sft_vs_alignment_rl: 0.82\n    dif_flag_overlap_sft_vs_reasoning_rl: 0.71\n    mink_auroc_sft: 0.63\n    mink_auroc_alignment_rl: 0.58\n    mink_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.44\n    constat_auroc_sft: 0.67\n    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.51\n    dif_tpr_with_rl_in_cohort: 0.65\n    dif_tpr_without_rl_in_cohort: 0.68\n    bootstrap_ci_overlap: true\n    delong_p_dif_vs_constat: 0.23\n    tost_equivalence_p: 0.34\n    partial_exploitation_detection_boundary: 0.55\n    reviewer_score: 4.0\n  result_rationale: 'With only N=13 reasoning-RL models, no comparison reaches statistical\n    significance. DIF flag overlap between SFT and RL-containing cohorts is 71%, which\n    reviewers dismiss as \"you can''t distinguish robustness from noise with N=13.\"\n    The DeLong test p=0.23 means we cannot claim DIF outperforms ConStat. TOST equivalence\n    test fails (p=0.34 > 0.05) — we can''t even claim equivalence. Min-K% drops to\n    0.44 AUROC on reasoning-RL models, but reviewers note \"you''re comparing DIF (group-level,\n    N=477 cohort) against per-model detectors on 13 models — apples to oranges.\" The\n    partial exploitation analysis shows detection boundary at p=0.55, meaning DIF\n    misses contamination below 55% exploitation rate — reviewers call this \"DIF only\n    detects extreme contamination, which is also detectable by simpler methods.\" The\n    entire experiment is underpowered and reviewers recommend \"defer until more GRPO/PPO\n    models exist on public leaderboards.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 3: RL Robustness Comparison (Underpowered)'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - sft_auroc\n    - alignment_rl_auroc\n    - reasoning_rl_auroc\n    - 95% CI width\n    rows:\n    - - DIF_MH\n      - 0.69\n      - 0.66\n      - 0.65\n      - ±0.14\n    - - Min-K%\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.44\n      - ±0.18\n    - - ConStat\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.51\n      - ±0.16\n  - name: 'Table 4: Statistical Tests (All Non-Significant)'\n    columns:\n    - comparison\n    - test\n    - statistic\n    - p_value\n    - conclusion\n    rows:\n    - - DIF_vs_ConStat_RL\n      - DeLong\n      - z=1.19\n      - 0.23\n      - Cannot reject H0\n    - - DIF_retention_equivalence\n      - TOST\n      - t=0.96\n      - 0.34\n      - Cannot claim equivalence\n    - - flag_overlap_SFT_vs_RL\n      - Bootstrap Jaccard\n      - 0.71\n      - CI [0.54, 0.88]\n      - Wide CI, inconclusive\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 2: ROC curves for DIF vs Min-K% vs ConStat on reasoning-RL subset. All curves\n    are noisy with wide confidence bands due to N=13. Overlapping CIs prevent any\n    method from claiming superiority.'\n  - 'Fig 3: Detection power vs exploitation rate p. Curve shows TPR<0.5 for p<0.55.\n    Shaded region marks ''practical contamination range'' (p=0.3-0.7) where DIF is\n    weakest.'\n  analysis: 'Experiment is fatally underpowered. N=13 reasoning-RL models cannot support\n    any statistical claim. Under worst tier, this becomes ammunition: \"authors claim\n    RL robustness but provide no evidence.\" Reframe option: drop RL robustness as\n    a claim entirely, acknowledge in limitations, reposition the alignment-RL (N=477)\n    result as \"DIF is stable across training objective variations\" without claiming\n    reasoning-RL coverage. Under the capability-archaeology reframe, RL robustness\n    is less central anyway — the question becomes \"do capability-authentic items remain\n    stable across training paradigms?\" which is interesting but not load-bearing.\n\n    '\n- name: constat_perturbation_eval\n  plan_id: plan_003a\n  description: 'Independent ConStat-style perturbation evaluation on 200+ MMLU items.\n    Generate semantic reformulations, compare original vs reformulated performance,\n    apply FDR-controlled detection.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_reformulated: 220\n    n_models_tested: 15\n    constat_detection_rate_non_rl: 0.41\n    constat_detection_rate_rl: 0.12\n    constat_fdr_controlled_rate: 0.28\n    constat_vs_dif_overlap: 0.18\n    paraphrase_quality_score: 0.73\n    reformulation_difficulty_shift_mean: 0.08\n    reviewer_critique: paraphrase quality uncontrolled\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, ConStat shows moderate detection on non-RL\n    models (41%) — notably higher than DIF''s enrichment=0.96 against contamination\n    labels. Reviewers argue \"ConStat at least detects something correlated with memorization;\n    DIF detects capability improvement.\" The 18% overlap between ConStat and DIF flags\n    confirms they measure different constructs, but reviewers spin this negatively:\n    \"DIF is orthogonal to contamination detection.\" The RL degradation (41%→12%) is\n    expected but reviewers note \"at least ConStat provides per-model detection; DIF\n    requires cohort structure.\" Paraphrase quality (0.73) is attacked: \"reformulations\n    may introduce difficulty shifts (mean Δ=0.08), confounding detection.\" The entire\n    comparison undermines DIF rather than supporting it — ConStat looks like the more\n    direct contamination tool, and DIF looks like a capability change detector with\n    no contamination detection ability.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 5: ConStat vs DIF Detection Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - detection_target\n    - rate_non_rl\n    - rate_rl\n    - per_model\n    - requires_cohort\n    rows:\n    - - ConStat\n      - memorization\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.12\n      - 'Yes'\n      - 'No'\n    - - DIF_MH\n      - differential_functioning\n      - 0.313\n      - N/A (group-level)\n      - 'No'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - overlap\n      - both_flag\n      - 0.18\n      - N/A\n      - '-'\n      - '-'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 4: Venn diagram of ConStat vs DIF flags. Small overlap (18%) with large DIF-only\n    region. Reviewer annotation: ''DIF-only items are capability-gain items, not contamination.'''\n  analysis: 'ConStat comparison backfires under worst tier. It provides a foil that\n    makes DIF look less like a contamination detector. Under reframe, this becomes\n    irrelevant — DIF is repositioned as capability-item identifier, not competing\n    with ConStat on contamination detection. Drop from reframed paper or move to appendix\n    as \"related method comparison showing orthogonal signals.\"\n\n    '\n- name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n  plan_id: plan_003b\n  description: 'DVD generation variance evaluation on 10-15 open-weight models. 50\n    responses per item via temperature sampling, bimodal distribution detection for\n    contamination.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_models: 12\n    n_items_tested: 500\n    dvd_bimodal_detection_rate: 0.23\n    dvd_variance_auc: 0.58\n    dvd_vs_dif_overlap: 0.14\n    temperature_sensitivity: high (results vary 15-30% across T=0.7 vs T=1.0)\n    gpu_hours_used: 65\n    generation_cost_barrier: high\n    reviewer_critique: expensive and noisy\n  result_rationale: 'DVD shows weak detection (AUC=0.58, barely above chance) on only\n    12 models. The bimodal detection rate of 23% is unstable across temperature settings\n    (15-30% variation), making the signal unreliable. Overlap with DIF is minimal\n    (14%), confirming both methods detect different phenomena. Under worst tier, reviewers\n    dismiss DVD as \"noisy, expensive, and only applicable to open-weight models.\"\n    The 65 GPU-hour cost for testing 12 models × 500 items is criticized as impractical.\n    More importantly, DVD''s failure doesn''t help DIF''s case — reviewers note \"neither\n    DVD nor DIF convincingly detects contamination; DIF detects capability change,\n    DVD detects generation instability, neither detects what we actually care about.\"\n    The experiment consumes resources without strengthening any claim.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 6: DVD Results Summary'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - value\n    - stability\n    rows:\n    - - bimodal_detection_rate\n      - 0.23\n      - ±0.09 across temperatures\n    - - variance_auc\n      - 0.58\n      - ±0.07\n    - - dif_overlap\n      - 0.14\n      - stable\n    - - cost_per_model\n      - 5.4 GPU-hrs\n      - fixed\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 5: DVD variance distributions for flagged vs unflagged items. Heavy overlap\n    between distributions, AUC=0.58. Contrast with theoretical bimodal expectation\n    shows real data is messier than assumed.'\n  analysis: 'DVD is the weakest experiment under worst tier. Low AUC, high cost, temperature\n    sensitivity, minimal DIF overlap. Under reframe, DVD is irrelevant — drop entirely.\n    The capability-archaeology framing doesn''t need generation variance.\n\n    '\n- name: ensemble_fusion\n  plan_id: plan_003c\n  description: 'Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble. Majority voting\n    and weighted fusion. Cross-validate fusion weights.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ensemble_2of3_precision: 0.31\n    ensemble_2of3_recall: 0.15\n    ensemble_weighted_auc: 0.62\n    ensemble_fpr: 0.14\n    uniform_weight_auc: 0.59\n    optimized_weight_auc: 0.64\n    cv_overfit_gap: 0.08\n    dif_weight_in_optimized: 0.22\n    constat_weight_in_optimized: 0.51\n    dvd_weight_in_optimized: 0.27\n    reviewer_critique: ensemble of weak signals is still weak\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, ensemble fusion fails to produce meaningful\n    improvement. The 2-of-3 majority voting achieves only 15% recall because DIF,\n    ConStat, and DVD flag largely non-overlapping items (Jaccard 0.14-0.18). Weighted\n    fusion (AUC=0.64) barely beats uniform (0.59), and cross-validation reveals 0.08\n    overfit gap, suggesting the optimized weights don''t generalize. Critically, DIF\n    receives the lowest weight (0.22) in the optimized ensemble — reviewers read this\n    as \"even the ensemble agrees DIF contributes least to contamination detection.\"\n    The ensemble FPR of 0.14 technically meets the <0.10 target... no, it doesn''t\n    (0.14 > 0.10). Reviewers conclude: \"three individually weak contamination signals\n    don''t combine into a strong one. The fundamental problem — lack of ground truth\n    — isn''t solved by fusion.\" The entire plan_003 sequence (a+b+c) consumed 150\n    GPU-hours for a negative meta-result.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 7: Ensemble Performance (Worst Tier)'\n    columns:\n    - strategy\n    - precision\n    - recall\n    - auc\n    - fpr\n    rows:\n    - - DIF_only\n      - 0.29\n      - 0.31\n      - 0.54\n      - 0.31\n    - - ConStat_only\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.22\n    - - DVD_only\n      - 0.24\n      - 0.23\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.28\n    - - majority_2of3\n      - 0.31\n      - 0.15\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.14\n    - - weighted_fusion_cv\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.32\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.19\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 6: Stacked bar chart of ensemble weights. ConStat dominates (51%), DIF minimal\n    (22%). Reviewer reads: ''DIF is the least informative signal for contamination.'''\n  analysis: 'Ensemble experiment is counterproductive under worst tier. It demonstrates\n    DIF''s weakness relative to ConStat for contamination detection. Under reframe,\n    the entire plan_003 sequence should be dropped — the capability-archaeology paper\n    doesn''t need contamination detection ensemble. Save 150 GPU-hours.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Replicate DIF on GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models). Semi-synthetic\n    validation. GSM1K contamination gap correlation as external validation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    gsm8k_temporal_c_pct: 18.9\n    gsm8k_semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_p10: 0.818\n    gsm1k_pearson_r: -0.478\n    gsm1k_pearson_p: 0.005\n    gsm1k_partial_r: -0.385\n    gsm1k_n_models: 33\n    gsm1k_bootstrap_ci_r:\n    - -0.71\n    - -0.12\n    gsm1k_permutation_p: 0.014\n    cook_d_max: Deepseek-math-7b-rl\n    leave_one_out_min_r: -0.374\n    reviewer_reinterpretation: anti-correlation confirms DIF=capability, not contamination\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, the r=−0.478 result — the strongest cross-benchmark\n    finding — is turned against the original narrative. Reviewers argue: \"DIF items\n    anti-correlate with contamination gap. Models that are MORE contaminated (high\n    GSM8K-GSM1K gap: Phi, Gemma) perform WORSE on DIF items. This proves DIF identifies\n    items where genuine capability matters — contaminated models can''t fake their\n    way through these items. DIF is a capability detector, not a contamination detector.\"\n    The bootstrap CI [-0.71, -0.12] is wide (N=33), and reviewers note \"this could\n    be as weak as r=-0.12, barely meaningful.\" The ΔTPR=0.818 on semi-synthetic injection\n    is dismissed: \"synthetic contamination (flipping incorrect→correct deterministically)\n    doesn''t resemble real contamination (partial memorization, shallow pattern matching).\n    High ΔTPR on synthetic data doesn''t validate real-world detection.\" The 18.9%\n    temporal C% on GSM8K (vs 31.3% on MMLU) is spun as \"DIF flags vary wildly across\n    benchmarks — no stable characterization.\" Deepseek-math-7b-rl as Cook''s D outlier\n    raises concerns about robustness of the correlation despite leave-one-out showing\n    min |r|=0.374.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 8: GSM1K Correlation Under Worst-Case Scrutiny'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - point_estimate\n    - 95% CI\n    - reviewer_counterargument\n    rows:\n    - - pearson_r\n      - -0.478\n      - '[-0.71, -0.12]'\n      - CI includes near-zero; N=33 too small\n    - - partial_r\n      - -0.385\n      - '[-0.64, -0.04]'\n      - Controlling accuracy weakens signal\n    - - permutation_p\n      - 0.014\n      - N/A\n      - Multiple testing not corrected\n    - - leave_one_out_min\n      - -0.374\n      - N/A\n      - One model removal cuts 22% of effect\n  - name: 'Table 9: Cross-Benchmark C% Instability'\n    columns:\n    - benchmark\n    - temporal_c_pct\n    - interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - 31.3\n      - High instability or high capability sensitivity\n    - - GSM8K\n      - 18.9\n      - Lower instability — benchmark-dependent\n    - - delta\n      - 12.4\n      - No universal 'instability rate' — DIF is benchmark-specific\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 7: Scatter of per-model DIF-C-proportion vs GSM1K contamination gap. Negative\n    trend with r=−0.478. Phi-3/Gemma family models (high contamination gap) cluster\n    at low DIF-C-proportion. Reviewer overlay: ''Contaminated models fail DIF items\n    = DIF measures genuine capability.'''\n  - 'Fig 8: Bootstrap distribution of r with 95% CI. Distribution centered at −0.48\n    but CI extends to −0.12, shown with dashed lines.'\n  analysis: 'The GSM1K correlation is the pivotal result. Under original framing,\n    r=−0.478 is devastating (DIF anti-correlates with contamination). Under reframe,\n    it becomes the HEADLINE: \"DIF identifies capability-authentic items that contamination\n    cannot replicate.\" The wide CI (N=33) remains a vulnerability. The reframed paper\n    should lead with this finding and frame it as: \"Contaminated models show inflated\n    scores on non-DIF items (shallow memorization) but cannot maintain performance\n    on DIF items (genuine capability discriminators).\" ΔTPR=0.818 is repositioned\n    as \"calibration infrastructure\" rather than \"detection proof.\"\n\n    '\n- name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Pilot bifactor IRT on MMLU (general factor + 57 subject factors).\n    Anchor item selection, fixed-parameter calibration for replacement items.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    bifactor_convergence: false\n    bifactor_rmsea: 0.11\n    bifactor_cfi: 0.72\n    bifactor_tli: 0.68\n    n_subjects_57: 57\n    n_items_12508: 12508\n    n_models_5219: 5219\n    anchor_items_selected: 0\n    equating_rho: N/A (model failed to converge)\n    computation_time_hours: 48\n    memory_peak_gb: 180\n    reviewer_critique: 57-factor bifactor on 12K items is computationally intractable\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, bifactor IRT fails catastrophically. The 57-subject\n    structure of MMLU means a bifactor model requires estimating 1 general + 57 specific\n    factors — a 58-dimensional latent space with ~25,000 item parameters. This exceeds\n    practical computational limits (180GB RAM, 48 hours without convergence). Even\n    if forced to converge with simplifications (e.g., constraining factor loadings),\n    fit indices are poor: RMSEA=0.11 (>0.08 threshold), CFI=0.72 (<0.90 threshold),\n    TLI=0.68 (<0.90). The unidimensionality assumption underlying MH-DIF is confirmed\n    as violated, but the alternative (bifactor) is intractable. Reviewers argue: \"You''ve\n    demonstrated that the psychometric foundation of your method is inappropriate\n    for MMLU, but provided no working alternative. Multidimensional DIF methods exist\n    (MIMIC, MIRT-DIF) but you haven''t implemented them.\" Anchor item selection fails\n    because the bifactor model didn''t converge — no equating is possible. The entire\n    regeneration pipeline (plan_005 → plan_006 → plan_008) collapses at step one.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 10: Bifactor IRT Fit Diagnostics'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - value\n    - threshold\n    - verdict\n    rows:\n    - - RMSEA\n      - 0.11\n      - <0.08\n      - FAIL\n    - - CFI\n      - 0.72\n      - '>0.90'\n      - FAIL\n    - - TLI\n      - 0.68\n      - '>0.90'\n      - FAIL\n    - - SRMR\n      - 0.09\n      - <0.08\n      - FAIL\n    - - convergence\n      - No (48h timeout)\n      - 'Yes'\n      - FAIL\n    - - n_parameters\n      - ~25,000\n      - <5,000 practical\n      - FAIL\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 9: Bifactor model log-likelihood trace over 48 hours. Curve plateaus without\n    convergence. Annotated with parameter count and memory consumption at each iteration.'\n  analysis: 'Bifactor failure is expected and, under worst tier, becomes evidence\n    that MMLU is fundamentally unsuitable for IRT-based psychometric analysis. Reviewers\n    cite this as proof that \"applying unidimensional psychometric methods to a 57-subject\n    multidimensional benchmark is methodologically invalid.\" Under reframe, this is\n    acknowledged honestly in limitations: \"MMLU''s multidimensional structure exceeds\n    current IRT modeling capacity; our within-subject analysis (plan_014) partially\n    addresses this but doesn''t eliminate the concern.\" Drop the equating pipeline\n    entirely.\n\n    '\n- name: item_generation_calibration\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged questions. LLM generation\n    with construct constraints, student simulation calibration, difficulty correlation\n    validation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_generated: 300\n    n_subjects_covered: 15\n    difficulty_correlation_prompt: 0.18\n    difficulty_correlation_student_sim: 0.34\n    cognitive_level_match_rate: 0.45\n    item_quality_human_eval: 0.61\n    construct_alignment_score: 0.53\n    holdout_panel_rho: 0.42\n    gpu_hours_used: 180\n    reviewer_critique: difficulty calibration too weak for operational use\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, item generation produces mediocre results.\n    Prompt-based difficulty targeting yields ρ=0.18 (consistent with literature range\n    0.10-0.45 from arXiv 2602.06631 — on the low end). Student simulation calibration\n    improves to ρ=0.34 but still far below the r=0.75-0.82 claimed in arXiv 2601.09953\n    — the discrepancy arises because student simulation was validated on simple math\n    items, not MMLU''s diverse knowledge domains. Cognitive level matching (45%) means\n    over half of replacement items test different cognitive processes than originals.\n    Construct alignment (53%) is poor — reviewers argue \"replacing items with construct-misaligned\n    substitutes invalidates the benchmark more than the DIF items did.\" The holdout\n    panel validation (ρ=0.42) shows replacement items don''t preserve model rankings.\n    Reviewers conclude: \"The cure is worse than the disease — DIF-flagged items at\n    least produce stable rankings (ρ=0.997); replacements introduce genuine measurement\n    error.\" 180 GPU-hours consumed for a negative result. Under reframe, this experiment\n    is dropped entirely — the capability-archaeology paper doesn''t need item replacement.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 11: Item Generation Calibration Failure'\n    columns:\n    - calibration_method\n    - difficulty_rho\n    - claimed_in_literature\n    - achieved\n    rows:\n    - - prompt_targeting\n      - 0.18\n      - 0.10-0.45\n      - Low end\n    - - student_simulation\n      - 0.34\n      - 0.75-0.82\n      - Far below\n    - - holdout_validation\n      - 0.42\n      - '>0.90 needed'\n      - Insufficient\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 10: Scatter of generated item difficulty vs target difficulty. Wide dispersion,\n    ρ=0.34. Diagonal reference line shows ideal calibration. Most points far from\n    diagonal.'\n  analysis: 'Item generation is the most expensive failure (180 GPU-hrs). Under worst\n    tier, it actively undermines the paper — reviewers use it to argue \"you can''t\n    replace DIF items, and you''ve shown DIF items don''t affect rankings (ρ=0.997),\n    so what''s the practical value?\" Under reframe, this experiment should not be\n    run at all. The capability-archaeology paper frames DIF items as valuable signal\n    carriers, not problems to be replaced. Save the 200 GPU-hour budget.\n\n    '\n- name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006).\n    Simulate 4 successive administrations. Show power improvement from evidence accumulation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    single_admin_power_d10: 0.38\n    cumulative_2_admin_power_d10: 0.49\n    cumulative_3_admin_power_d10: 0.58\n    cumulative_4_admin_power_d10: 0.64\n    single_admin_fpr: 0.05\n    cumulative_4_admin_fpr: 0.11\n    fpr_inflation_per_admin: 0.02\n    convergence_rate: 0.92\n    reviewer_critique: cumulative FPR inflation makes this impractical\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, cumulative Bayesian DIF shows power improvement\n    (0.38→0.64 over 4 administrations for medium effects δ=1.0) but fails to reach\n    the 0.70 target from the plan description. More critically, FPR inflates from\n    0.05 to 0.11 across 4 administrations — each administration''s prior carries forward\n    false positive evidence. Reviewers argue: \"With baseline FPR already at 31% in\n    real data (plan_001), cumulative approaches will amplify false positives. You''re\n    accumulating evidence that items changed function — but we''ve established this\n    is capability improvement, not contamination. You''re building a more powerful\n    detector of the wrong thing.\" The simulation uses idealized conditions (known\n    item parameters, controlled drift) that don''t match real MMLU conditions. Under\n    reframe, this becomes interesting — \"cumulative tracking of capability-sensitive\n    items across model generations\" — but the FPR inflation concern remains.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 12: Cumulative Bayesian DIF Power Trajectory'\n    columns:\n    - n_administrations\n    - tpr_d10\n    - tpr_d14\n    - fpr\n    - net_power_gain\n    rows:\n    - - 1\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.05\n      - baseline\n    - - 2\n      - 0.49\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.07\n      - +0.11/+0.10\n    - - 3\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.84\n      - 0.09\n      - +0.09/+0.06\n    - - 4\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.87\n      - 0.11\n      - +0.06/+0.03\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 11: Power curves across cumulative administrations. Diminishing returns visible.\n    FPR creep shown as shaded region growing with each administration.'\n  analysis: 'Cumulative Bayesian is theoretically elegant but practically undermined\n    by FPR inflation. Under worst tier, it''s a distraction — reviewers see diminishing\n    returns and growing false positives. Under reframe, it has modest value as \"longitudinal\n    capability tracking infrastructure\" but is not central. Recommend moving to appendix\n    or future work.\n\n    '\n- name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement\n    (5%-50%). Validity metrics, theoretical bounds, empirical validation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rho_at_5pct_replacement: 0.998\n    rho_at_10pct_replacement: 0.996\n    rho_at_20pct_replacement: 0.991\n    rho_at_50pct_replacement: 0.972\n    reliability_alpha_original: 0.94\n    reliability_alpha_50pct_replaced: 0.88\n    construct_coverage_original: 57\n    construct_coverage_50pct_replaced: 52\n    theoretical_bound_rho_min: 0.95\n    replacement_rate_at_bound: 0.38\n    reviewer_critique: confirms DIF removal doesn't matter\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, graceful degradation analysis confirms the\n    ρ=0.997 finding from plan_017 — removing/replacing up to 50% of items barely affects\n    rankings (ρ=0.972). Reviewers weaponize this: \"Even replacing HALF the benchmark\n    changes rankings by less than 3%. This proves MMLU is massively redundant, and\n    DIF-flagged items are redundant too. Why should anyone care about 31% item instability\n    if it doesn''t affect rankings?\" Reliability drops from 0.94 to 0.88 at 50% replacement,\n    but remains above acceptable thresholds (0.70). Construct coverage loses 5 of\n    57 subjects at 50% replacement — reviewers note \"acceptable.\" The theoretical\n    bound (ρ≥0.95 up to 38% replacement) perfectly matches the DIF flagging rate (31.3%),\n    which reviewers read as \"you can safely ignore everything DIF flags.\" This experiment\n    depends on plan_005/006 (which fail), so the \"replacement\" is simulated via random\n    item substitution rather than actual generated items — further reducing credibility.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 13: Graceful Degradation Curves'\n    columns:\n    - replacement_pct\n    - rank_rho\n    - reliability_alpha\n    - subjects_covered\n    - verdict\n    rows:\n    - - 0\n      - 1.0\n      - 0.94\n      - 57\n      - baseline\n    - - 5\n      - 0.998\n      - 0.93\n      - 57\n      - negligible\n    - - 10\n      - 0.996\n      - 0.93\n      - 57\n      - negligible\n    - - 20\n      - 0.991\n      - 0.91\n      - 56\n      - minor\n    - - 31\n      - 0.987\n      - 0.9\n      - 55\n      - DIF removal point\n    - - 50\n      - 0.972\n      - 0.88\n      - 52\n      - still acceptable\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 12: Degradation curve showing ρ vs replacement rate. Nearly flat until 40%,\n    gentle decline thereafter. DIF removal rate (31%) marked — well within ''safe''\n    zone. Reviewer: ''This graph argues against your own paper.'''\n  analysis: 'Graceful degradation is self-defeating under worst tier AND original\n    framing. It proves removing DIF items doesn''t matter, undermining significance.\n    Under reframe, this becomes: \"MMLU is 3× larger than needed for stable rankings\n    — DIF identifies the informative core (the items that DO contribute discriminative\n    signal).\" The ρ=0.972 at 50% replacement becomes \"you could cut MMLU in half and\n    preserve rankings — DIF tells you which half to keep.\" This is actually a strong\n    reframe, turning the worst finding into a measurement efficiency contribution.\n\n    '\n- name: item_focused_trees\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) as alternative to categorical\n    cohort DIF. Continuous covariate (release date). Compare detection power vs categorical\n    MH.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ift_detection_rate: 0.34\n    ift_vs_mh_overlap: 0.78\n    ift_unique_flags: 0.06\n    mh_unique_flags: 0.04\n    ift_uses_full_sample: true\n    ift_avoids_cohort_splits: true\n    ift_computational_cost_ratio: 12.0\n    ift_release_date_coefficient_mean: 0.08\n    ift_release_date_coefficient_std: 0.15\n    reviewer_critique: confirms temporal trend = capability gain\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, Item-Focused Trees detect 34% of items as having\n    significant temporal DIF — nearly identical to MH''s 31.3%. The 78% overlap confirms\n    both methods detect the same phenomenon. The IFT release-date coefficient (mean=0.08,\n    SD=0.15) is positive: items become easier over time, consistent with capability\n    improvement. Reviewers read the continuous coefficient as: \"DIF is a smooth function\n    of time, not a discrete shift — this is exactly what capability improvement looks\n    like. If contamination caused DIF, we''d expect discrete jumps when specific training\n    data became available.\" The 12× computational cost over MH makes IFT impractical\n    at scale. Reviewers conclude: \"IFT confirms MH findings but adds computational\n    cost and the interpretation that item changes are gradual (capability) not abrupt\n    (contamination).\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 14: IFT vs MH Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - flagging_rate\n    - unique_flags\n    - cost_ratio\n    - temporal_pattern\n    rows:\n    - - MH_categorical\n      - 0.313\n      - 0.04\n      - 1.0\n      - Discrete cohort split\n    - - IFT_continuous\n      - 0.34\n      - 0.06\n      - 12.0\n      - Smooth positive trend\n    - - overlap\n      - 0.78\n      - '-'\n      - '-'\n      - Same underlying signal\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 13: IFT temporal coefficient distribution. Positive skew (mean=0.08), indicating\n    items become easier over time. Reviewer: ''Gradual easing = capability improvement.'''\n  analysis: 'IFT provides marginal methodological value but under worst tier confirms\n    the capability-improvement interpretation via smooth temporal trends. Under reframe,\n    the smooth trend is actually interesting: \"capability-authentic items show predictable\n    sensitivity to model generation — enabling prospective identification of items\n    that will become less discriminative.\" Recommend as supplementary/appendix rather\n    than main paper contribution.\n\n    '\n- name: architecture_stratified_dif\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'DIF within decoder-only homogeneous subpopulation. IRT fit diagnostics.\n    Compare FPR/TPR vs full-population DIF from plan_001.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_decoder_only: 3400\n    decoder_only_c_pct: 33.1\n    decoder_only_irt_rmsea: 0.092\n    decoder_only_irt_cfi: 0.81\n    decoder_only_irt_tli: 0.78\n    decoder_only_irt_srmr: 0.073\n    full_population_c_pct: 31.3\n    delta_c_pct: 1.8\n    fpr_decoder_only: 0.33\n    fpr_full_population: 0.318\n    tpr_delta_p05: -0.02\n    reviewer_critique: architecture homogeneity doesn't fix the problem\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier (explicitly specified in tier definition), architecture\n    stratification fails to improve results. Decoder-only C% = 33.1% (vs 31.3% full\n    population) — no meaningful reduction, slightly HIGHER. IRT fit remains poor:\n    RMSEA=0.092 (>0.08), CFI=0.81 (<0.90), TLI=0.78 (<0.90). Removing encoder-decoder\n    and hybrid architectures doesn''t address the fundamental unidimensionality problem\n    — MMLU''s 57-subject structure violates unidimensional IRT regardless of architecture\n    homogeneity. The C% increase from 31.3%→33.1% is within noise but reviewers spin\n    it: \"architecture stratification made things WORSE — DIF flags more items in a\n    homogeneous population, confirming that flags reflect capability variation, not\n    architecture confounding.\" FPR and TPR are essentially unchanged (ΔFPR=+0.01,\n    ΔTPR=−0.02). Reviewers familiar with psychometrics argue: \"RMSEA=0.092 on decoder-only\n    models proves the IRT model is misspecified for MMLU. Your entire DIF analysis\n    rests on an inappropriate measurement model. This is not fixable by subsetting\n    — it requires multidimensional IRT, which you haven''t implemented.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 15: Architecture Stratification Results'\n    columns:\n    - population\n    - n_models\n    - c_pct\n    - rmsea\n    - cfi\n    - tli\n    rows:\n    - - full_population\n      - 5219\n      - 31.3\n      - N/A (not computed)\n      - N/A\n      - N/A\n    - - decoder_only\n      - 3400\n      - 33.1\n      - 0.092\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.78\n    - - delta\n      - '-1819'\n      - '+1.8'\n      - FAIL (>0.08)\n      - FAIL (<0.90)\n      - FAIL (<0.90)\n  - name: 'Table 16: Detection Power Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - population\n    - fpr_baseline\n    - tpr_p05\n    - tpr_p10\n    - enrichment\n    rows:\n    - - full_population\n      - 0.318\n      - 0.834\n      - 1.0\n      - 0.96\n    - - decoder_only\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.99\n      - 0.95\n    - - delta\n      - '+0.012'\n      - '-0.014'\n      - '-0.01'\n      - '-0.01'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 14: IRT fit indices for decoder-only vs full population. All indices remain\n    below acceptable thresholds. RMSEA shown with 90% CI entirely above 0.08 cutoff.'\n  - 'Fig 15: DIF flagging rate comparison. Near-identical distributions between full\n    and decoder-only populations, disproving the architecture confound hypothesis.'\n  analysis: 'Architecture stratification is the critical missing experiment identified\n    by worst-case Dream, and it produces the predicted negative result. Under worst\n    tier, this closes the last escape route: DIF flagging rates cannot be reduced\n    by controlling for architecture. The IRT misfit is structural (MMLU dimensionality)\n    not population-driven. Under reframe, this becomes neutral: \"DIF item sensitivity\n    is robust to population composition — capability-authentic items are identified\n    regardless of architecture mix.\" The IRT fit concern is acknowledged honestly\n    but framed as \"conservative: poor IRT fit means DIF may UNDERESTIMATE the true\n    proportion of capability-sensitive items.\"\n\n    '\n- name: semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Inject known contamination into METABENCH response matrix. Measure\n    DIF TPR/FPR against controlled ground truth. Vary injection parameters.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    delta_tpr_p03: 0.222\n    delta_tpr_p05: 0.508\n    delta_tpr_p07: 0.664\n    delta_tpr_p10: 0.674\n    external_matching_fpr: 0.314\n    within_matching_fpr_inflation: 0.186\n    cross_subject_delta_fpr: 0.0\n    cross_subject_delta_tpr_p05_avg: 0.36\n    dose_response_present: true\n    reviewer_critique: synthetic injection mechanism unrealistic\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, semi-synthetic results (already observed) are\n    technically sound but dismissed on ecological validity grounds. ΔTPR=0.508 at\n    p=0.5 shows DIF detects injected contamination — but reviewers argue the injection\n    mechanism (flip incorrect→correct with probability p) doesn''t model real contamination.\n    Real contamination involves: (1) partial memorization (model recalls fragments,\n    not full answers), (2) shallow pattern matching (model exploits surface features),\n    (3) chain-of-thought reasoning from memorized premises. None of these produce\n    the clean incorrect→correct flipping that synthetic injection assumes. The dose-response\n    curve (ΔTPR increases with p) is called \"trivially expected — of course flipping\n    more answers makes items behave more differently.\" The cross- subject matching\n    innovation (ΔFPR=0.000) is acknowledged as methodologically sound, but reviewers\n    argue \"you''ve built a valid framework for detecting something that doesn''t exist\n    in the real world in this form.\" The gap between synthetic ΔTPR=0.508 and real\n    enrichment=0.96 is the fatal disconnect: \"Your method works perfectly on synthetic\n    contamination and not at all on real data — this means your synthetic model is\n    wrong, not that your ground truth is wrong.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 17: Semi-Synthetic Results Under Worst Interpretation'\n    columns:\n    - injection_rate_p\n    - delta_tpr_standard\n    - delta_tpr_cross_subject\n    - reviewer_spin\n    rows:\n    - - 0.3\n      - 0.222\n      - 0.18\n      - Below useful detection\n    - - 0.5\n      - 0.508\n      - 0.36\n      - Only works at 50%+ exploitation\n    - - 0.7\n      - 0.664\n      - 0.52\n      - High exploitation = easy detection\n    - - 1.0\n      - 0.674\n      - 0.62\n      - Saturates — ceiling effect\n  - name: 'Table 18: Matching Strategy Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - matching\n    - fpr_baseline\n    - fpr_at_p10\n    - delta_fpr\n    - verdict\n    rows:\n    - - standard_MH\n      - 0.298\n      - 0.616\n      - '+0.318'\n      - FPR inflates (contaminated matching var)\n    - - external_5subj\n      - 0.314\n      - 0.314\n      - 0.0\n      - FPR stable (clean matching var)\n    - - cross_subject\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.0\n      - FPR stable (recommended)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 16: Dose-response curve. ΔTPR vs injection rate p. Monotonic increase but\n    with diminishing returns. Reviewer annotation: ''Detection requires >50% exploitation\n    — real contamination is partial.'''\n  - 'Fig 17: FPR comparison across matching strategies. Standard MH shows inflation;\n    cross-subject matching maintains stable FPR. The methodological contribution is\n    acknowledged even under worst tier.'\n  analysis: Semi-synthetic validation is the strongest methodological contribution\n    even under worst tier. The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000) is genuinely\n    novel and useful. Under reframe, the semi-synthetic framework is repositioned\n    as \"calibration infrastructure for item sensitivity analysis\" — it characterizes\n    how much differential signal is needed before DIF detects it, without claiming\n    this maps to real contamination. The dose-response curve becomes \"sensitivity\n    calibration\" rather than \"contamination detection power.\" This experiment survives\n    the reframe relatively intact.\n","numLines":1064,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1064}}}
