{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013p2sgCCeZKwJ4vZAREdAuh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SiAWUgkwVwS4ftuGRvtEHe","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report — Full Roundtable\n\n**Paper**: \"Identifying Capability-Authentic Benchmark Items via Differential Item Functioning\"\n**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers, 4-Step Roundtable)\n**Date**: 2026-05-21\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Independent Reviews\n\n### Reviewer 1 (R1) — Methods & Experiments | Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Summary**: The paper applies MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,219 × 12,508) and reports 31.3% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF. After the original contamination-detection hypothesis failed (enrichment ≈ 1.0), the paper reframes DIF-flagged items as \"capability-authentic.\" The semi-synthetic validation framework and cross-subject matching are competent methodological contributions, but several core claims rest on weak statistical foundations.\n\n**Strengths**:\n1. Scale and thoroughness — 12 experiments forming a coherent decision chain, the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks\n2. Semi-synthetic validation framework well-designed (dose-response, 5 seeds per condition, Fisher's exact test)\n3. Cross-subject matching as FPR control (ΔFPR=0.000) — clean, reusable methodological finding\n4. Sanity checks convincing (random split 0.18% vs temporal 31.3%)\n5. Honest reporting of negative results (enrichment ≈ 1.0, bifactor failure, underpowered RL)\n6. DIF removal causes MORE ranking disruption than random removal (z = −8 to −49σ) — non-obvious finding\n\n**Major Weaknesses**:\n1. **31% temporal C% is fundamentally uninterpretable** — cannot decompose into contamination vs. capability improvement vs. multidimensionality artifacts. Within-subject analysis does not resolve this. The headline number may reflect violated unidimensionality assumptions rather than genuine item-level anomalies.\n2. **GSM1K validation underpowered** — N=33, bootstrap CI [−0.71, −0.12], leave-one-out drops |r| by 22%. Partial r=−0.385 barely survives α=0.05. Possible difficulty confounding not controlled.\n3. **Baseline FPR of 31–53%** undermines practical utility. A diagnostic that flags one-third of items provides limited actionable guidance.\n4. **Semi-synthetic injection too simplistic** — deterministic incorrect→correct flipping doesn't model real contamination (partial memorization, shallow pattern matching). No sensitivity analysis on injection mechanism.\n5. **Unidimensionality violation acknowledged but not adequately addressed** — IRT fit indices all fail (RMSEA=0.092, CFI=0.81, TLI=0.78). MH-DIF matching on total score conflates between-dimension ability differences with item-level DIF on a 57-subject benchmark.\n\n**Moderate Weaknesses**:\n6. Architecture stratification (Exp 2) adds little — C% moves from 31.3% to 33.1%, within noise\n7. Cross-subject matching tested on only 5 of 57 subjects\n\n**Key Missing Analyses**:\n- Null-model simulation for expected C% under ability differences alone\n- Item difficulty confounding control in GSM1K correlation\n- SIBTEST or logistic regression DIF on subset (handles multidimensionality)\n- Temporal cutoff sensitivity analysis\n- DIF flag distribution across difficulty quantiles\n\n---\n\n### Reviewer 2 (R2) — Novelty & Related Work | Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Summary**: The paper applies an established psychometric technique (MH-DIF, 1959) unchanged to LLM benchmarks. The \"capability-authentic\" reframing is an interpretive contribution, not a methodological one. The ground truth meta-critique and cross-subject matching are genuine contributions. Related work is dangerously thin (9 references) and differentiation from Polo et al. (2024) Growing Pains is insufficient.\n\n**Strengths**:\n1. Scale unprecedented for psychometric DIF analysis\n2. Ground truth meta-critique — showing web-overlap labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination — is a real intellectual contribution with implications for Min-K%, ConStat, and all methods validating against web-overlap labels\n3. Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) — practical, immediately actionable\n4. GSM1K negative correlation (r=−0.478) is surprising and inverts naive expectations\n5. Honest negative result reporting prevents others from repeating failed approaches\n\n**Major Weaknesses**:\n1. **Methodological novelty limited** — MH-DIF applied unchanged from 1959/1988. \"Capability-authentic\" is interpretive reframing, not a new method. How does this advance beyond Polo et al. (2024)?\n2. **\"Capability-authentic\" reframing has circularity problem** — DIF selects items with largest cross-group differences (conditioned on ability). Removing them MUST disrupt rankings more than random removal. The z = −8 to −49σ result may be tautological. Authors need to show items selected by other criteria (highest variance, highest discrimination) do NOT produce equivalent disruption.\n3. **Related work dangerously thin — 9 references**. Missing critical citations:\n   - Lalor et al. (2019): foundational IRT-for-NLP work\n   - Shealy & Stout (1993): SIBTEST for separating DIF from impact\n   - Clauser & Mazor (1998): standard DIF review\n   - Wang et al. (ICLR 2026): RL fragility of contamination detection\n   - Balloccu et al. (EACL 2024): \"Leak, Cheat, Repeat\" contamination survey\n   - Golchin & Surdeanu (ICLR 2024): \"Time Travel in LLMs\"\n   - Needs ~30–35 more references total\n4. **Differentiation from Growing Pains unvalidated** — complementarity claim never empirically tested. Do DIF flags predict anchor-item drift in Growing Pains' fixed-parameter calibration?\n5. **Narrative is post-hoc rationalization** — pivoted from contamination detection → benchmark characterization → capability archaeology. Each pivot defensible individually, but final framing reads as salvaging positive-sounding results from negative experiments.\n\n**Moderate Weaknesses**:\n6. GSM1K statistical fragility (N=33, wide CI)\n7. 12 experiments create kitchen-sink impression — paper would benefit from ruthless pruning to 4–5 core experiments\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Cross-Sharing Reactions\n\n### R1's Reaction to R2\n\n**Agreements**: R2's circularity concern is the most substantive critique — the ablation (DIF vs. high-discrimination vs. high-variance removal) must be run. GSM1K bootstrap CIs should have been reported. Thin related work (9 references) is a genuine problem. Growing Pains complementarity should be empirically validated.\n\n**Disagreements**: R2 overstates the circularity — MH conditions on matched ability, so DIF flags are NOT simply items with largest raw group differences. A fairer test compares against items with highest raw accuracy gaps (no ability matching). \"Kitchen-sink\" criticism overstated given the paper's pivot to 4 focused claims. Calling the narrative \"post-hoc rationalization\" is uncharitable — pivots during analysis are normal science.\n\n**Score unchanged**: 4/6.\n\n### R2's Reaction to R1\n\n**Agreements**: 31% C% being fundamentally uninterpretable is the paper's central vulnerability. Unidimensionality violation (all IRT fit indices fail) compounds the novelty gap. Semi-synthetic simplicity and GSM1K underpowering are valid concerns.\n\n**Disagreements**: R1's \"baseline FPR of 31–53% undermines utility\" overstates the problem — under the diagnostic framing, a high flag rate IS the empirical finding, not a false positive problem. The random-split sanity check (0.18%) confirms real signal. R1's dismissal of architecture stratification is too hasty. R1's missing analyses list collectively amounts to requesting a different paper.\n\n**Score unchanged**: 4/6.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Convergence\n\n### Points of Agreement (Both Reviewers)\n\n1. **GSM1K validation is underpowered** (N=33, wide CI, sensitive to outliers) — this is the load-bearing external validation and it's fragile\n2. **Unidimensionality violation is a serious unresolved concern** — IRT fit fails all thresholds, and MMLU's 57-subject structure makes unidimensional MH-DIF questionable\n3. **31% C% cannot be decomposed** into contamination vs. capability vs. multidimensionality artifacts — the headline number is ambiguous\n4. **Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) is a genuine methodological contribution** — practical, reusable, well-demonstrated\n5. **Semi-synthetic validation framework is competent** but injection mechanism is oversimplified\n6. **Honest negative result reporting is commendable** — bifactor failure, underpowered RL, weak ensemble, failed item generation\n7. **Related work is far too thin** (9 references for a paper spanning psychometrics, contamination detection, benchmark design, and IRT)\n8. **Scale of analysis is impressive** — largest DIF study on LLM benchmarks\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n\n1. **\"FPR\" framing**: R1 sees 31% baseline flagging rate as undermining utility; R2 argues under diagnostic framing this IS the finding, not an error rate\n2. **Architecture stratification value**: R1 dismisses as adding little; R2 defends as methodologically informative\n3. **Circularity severity**: R2 sees ranking-disruption result as potentially tautological; R1 partially agrees but notes ability-conditioning makes it non-trivial\n4. **Post-hoc narrative**: R2 calls it rationalization; R1 considers it normal scientific pivot\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Overall Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept) — Borderline\n\nBoth reviewers independently converge on 4/6 and hold after cross-sharing.\n\n### Decision Rationale\n\n**For acceptance (what works)**:\n- The paper presents the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks with genuinely thorough empirical investigation\n- The ground truth meta-critique (web-overlap labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination, evidenced by enrichment ≈ 1.0) is a real intellectual contribution that has implications beyond this paper\n- Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) is a clean, reusable methodological contribution\n- The GSM1K anti-correlation (r=−0.478), despite being underpowered, is a surprising and informative finding\n- Honest reporting of 12 experiments including extensive negative results is exemplary and prevents wasted effort by others\n- The measurement efficiency finding (ρ=0.997 at 31% removal → MMLU is ~3× redundant) is practically useful\n\n**Against acceptance (what doesn't work)**:\n- The methodological novelty is limited — MH-DIF is applied unchanged from classical psychometrics. The \"capability-authentic\" reframing is interpretive, not methodological\n- The headline finding (31% C%) is fundamentally ambiguous — cannot separate capability improvement from multidimensionality artifacts from potential contamination\n- The unidimensionality assumption underlying MH-DIF is violated (all IRT fit indices fail standard thresholds), and no multidimensionality-aware alternative (SIBTEST, MIRT-DIF) is attempted\n- The GSM1K validation (N=33, CI [−0.71, −0.12]) is too underpowered to anchor the entire \"capability-authentic\" narrative\n- The circularity concern about ranking disruption (DIF selects items with largest conditioned group differences → their removal must disproportionately affect rankings) has not been addressed with appropriate ablations\n- Related work is severely underdeveloped (9 references; needs ~40 for venue standards)\n- The narrative shows signs of post-hoc reconstruction, though the individual findings are solid\n\n### Actionable Revision Recommendations (Priority-Ordered)\n\n1. **Run the circularity ablation** — Compare ranking disruption from removing DIF-C items vs. items selected by (a) highest raw accuracy gap, (b) highest item discrimination, (c) highest variance. If DIF-selected items produce uniquely large disruption beyond these baselines, the circularity concern is addressed.\n\n2. **Report bootstrap CIs for all key correlations** — GSM1K r=−0.478 needs explicit CI; partial r needs CI; domain ρ=0.307 needs CI. Be transparent about statistical fragility.\n\n3. **Attempt SIBTEST on a subject subset** — Even a preliminary analysis on 5–10 subjects using SIBTEST (which separates DIF from impact) would substantially address the unidimensionality concern.\n\n4. **Add 25–35 references** — Mandatory additions include Lalor et al. (2019), Shealy & Stout (1993), Clauser & Mazor (1998), Wang et al. (ICLR 2026), Balloccu et al. (EACL 2024). Position against the IRT-for-NLP and contamination detection literatures.\n\n5. **Empirically test Growing Pains complementarity** — Run DIF flags through Polo et al.'s fixed-parameter calibration framework. Show whether C-classified items predict anchor drift.\n\n6. **Prune to 5–6 core experiments** — Move DVD, ensemble fusion, cumulative Bayesian DIF, and item generation to appendix. Focus the main paper on: (1) core DIF characterization, (2) architecture stratification, (3) GSM1K validation, (4) semi-synthetic framework, (5) graceful degradation.\n\n7. **Add null-model calibration** — Simulate expected C% under a 2PL model where the focal group has higher θ. Establish whether 31% is anomalous or predicted.\n\n### Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nThe paper covers all 12 experiments from the design document:\n- plan_001 (real data DIF MMLU): ✓ Experiment 1\n- plan_002 (RL robustness): ✓ Experiment 8\n- plan_003a (ConStat): ✓ Experiment 9\n- plan_003b (DVD): ✓ Experiment 10\n- plan_003c (ensemble fusion): ✓ Experiment 11\n- plan_004 (GSM8K/GSM1K): ✓ Experiment 3\n- plan_005 (bifactor IRT): ✓ Experiment 12a\n- plan_006 (item generation): ✓ Experiment 12b\n- plan_007 (cumulative Bayesian): ✓ Experiment 7\n- plan_008 (graceful degradation): ✓ Experiment 5\n- plan_009 (IFT): ✓ Experiment 6\n- plan_010 (architecture stratification): ✓ Experiment 2\n- plan_011 (semi-synthetic): ✓ Experiment 4\n\nCoverage is complete. However, the paper would benefit from moving 5–6 weaker experiments to appendix.\n\n### Summary\n\nA thorough, honest, and impressively-scaled empirical study that applies classical psychometric DIF analysis to LLM benchmarks and discovers that DIF-flagged items concentrate discriminative capability signal rather than indicating contamination. The cross-subject matching innovation and ground truth meta-critique are genuine contributions. However, limited methodological novelty (unchanged MH-DIF), fundamental ambiguity in the headline finding (31% C% undecomposable), violated psychometric assumptions (unidimensionality), underpowered external validation (N=33 GSM1K), and severely thin related work place this at the borderline. With targeted revisions — particularly the circularity ablation, SIBTEST analysis, and expanded related work — this could reach Accept territory at a top venue.\n","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"worst","dream_id":"20260521T012912","overall_score":"4/6 Weak Accept (Borderline)","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":67444,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":713},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f077ac60-b5f3-45fc-82ab-a50de5ad3465","uuid":"b9406dad-e6ba-40b2-97e7-fd10631b7230"}
